export const SEARCH_ENTRIES: SearchEntry[] = [{"kind":"page","title":"Architecture","page":"Architecture","path":"/docs/architecture","text":"How podbox works — codegen pipeline, generated Containerfile and Quadlet units, host-guest socket protocol, UID mapping, and project structure."},{"kind":"section","title":"How It Works","page":"Architecture","path":"/docs/architecture#how-it-works","text":"A definition TOML is the single source of truth. Everything podbox generates — Containerfiles, Quadlet systemd units, lock files, desktop entries — derives from this one file. The user never writes a raw Containerfile or systemd unit manually."},{"kind":"section","title":"Codegen Pipeline","page":"Architecture","path":"/docs/architecture#codegen-pipeline","text":"podbox build runs these steps in order. Each codegen step is a pure function: data in, string out, no I/O. Orchestration (file writes, podman invocations) is separate."},{"kind":"section","title":"Generated Containerfile","page":"Architecture","path":"/docs/architecture#generated-containerfile","text":"FROM fedora:44  # [image.packages] RUN dnf install -y git gcc ripgrep && dnf clean all  # [image.run] custom steps RUN dnf clean all "},{"kind":"section","title":"Build Context Layout","page":"Architecture","path":"/docs/architecture#build-context-layout","text":"~/.local/share/podbox/<name>/ ├── Containerfile ├── podbox-guest          # static musl binary from host"},{"kind":"section","title":"Generated Quadlet Files","page":"Architecture","path":"/docs/architecture#generated-quadlet-files","text":"Files written to /.config/containers/systemd/:"},{"kind":"section","title":"myenv.build","page":"Architecture","path":"/docs/architecture#myenvbuild","text":"[Build] ImageTag=localhost/podbox-myenv:latest File=/home/user/.local/share/podbox/myenv/Containerfile The .build unit makes myenv.service depend on the build. Images are only rebuilt when the Containerfile changes."},{"kind":"section","title":"myenv.socket","page":"Architecture","path":"/docs/architecture#myenvsocket","text":"[Unit] Description=podbox host-guest socket — myenv  [Socket] ListenStream=%t/podbox/myenv.sock Service=myenv-host.service SocketMode=0600 DirectoryMode=0700 %t is systemd's specifier for $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR. The socket is created before the container starts and persists across restarts."},{"kind":"section","title":"myenv.container","page":"Architecture","path":"/docs/architecture#myenvcontainer","text":"Key Quadlet settings (see quadlet.md for full list): Setting Value Purpose · UserNS keep-id Maps host UID/GID into container · User root Run as root (UID mapped via UserNS) · SecurityLabelDisable true Required for Wayland socket access · NoNewPrivileges true Block setuid escalation (sudo, su) · PodmanArgs --init catatonit as PID 1 (zombie reaping) · PodmanArgs --workdir=/home/%u Default working directory · Volume /.flatpak-info:/.flatpak-info:ro Sandbox detection marker (portals) · Volume %h/containers/ :/home/%u:Z Isolated home (never the host home) · Volume %t/podbox/ .sock:%t/podbox/ .sock Host-guest socket · Environment HOST_USER, HOST_UID, HOST_GID Host identity injected · Environment PATH=/run/podbox/bin:… Interceptor directory prepended · Restart on-failure Auto-restart on crash Volumes for Wayland, audio, D-Bus, XDG dirs, GPU devices, and theme/icon/font sync are added conditiona"},{"kind":"section","title":"Host-Guest Socket Protocol","page":"Architecture","path":"/docs/architecture#host-guest-socket-protocol","text":"The guest daemon connects to a Unix socket on the host to bridge container capabilities. Messages are length-prefixed JSON (see protocol.md for the wire format)."},{"kind":"section","title":"Guest Daemon (podbox-guest)","page":"Architecture","path":"/docs/architecture#guest-daemon-podbox-guest","text":"The guest binary is a static musl binary baked into every built image. Its behavior is determined by argv[0]: Invoked as Mode · podbox-guest --entry Fork daemon, exec user shell/command · podbox-guest --daemon Event loop, interceptor setup · notify-send (symlink) Parse args, forward to daemon · xdg-open (symlink) Parse args, forward to daemon · podbox-clipboard (symlink) Read stdin / write stdout for clipboard · host-exec (symlink) Execute command on host, relay output"},{"kind":"section","title":"Daemon startup sequence","page":"Architecture","path":"/docs/architecture#daemon-startup-sequence","text":"Read PODBOX_CONTAINER env → derive socket paths · Create /run/podbox/bin/ directory · Check version drift — compare PODBOX_HOST_VERSION against podbox-guest version · Connect to host socket (3 retries × 500ms) · Handshake: send capabilities, receive accepted list and idle timeout · Install interceptor symlinks in /run/podbox/bin/ for accepted capabilities · Prepend /run/podbox/bin to $PATH via /etc/profile.d/podbox.sh and /etc/fish/conf.d/podbox.fish · Enter event loop (poll + pidfd-based, 0% CPU when idle, configurable idle timeout) If the socket is absent at startup, the daemon logs a warning and exits cleanly. The container continues running without integration — this is intentional."},{"kind":"section","title":"UID Mapping","page":"Architecture","path":"/docs/architecture#uid-mapping","text":"UserNS=keep-id + User=root creates an idmapped mount that shifts UIDs by 1 inside the container (host UID 1000 → container UID 999). The entrypoint reads the actual home owner and makes the directory world-writable. No chown is performed on bind-mounted directories — that would corrupt host ownership through the idmapped mount."},{"kind":"section","title":"Runtime Flow (Full Sequence)","page":"Architecture","path":"/docs/architecture#runtime-flow-full-sequence","text":""},{"kind":"section","title":"Project Structure","page":"Architecture","path":"/docs/architecture#project-structure","text":"podbox/ ├── Cargo.toml                    # workspace root ├── crates/ │   ├── podbox/                   # host CLI binary │   │   └── src/ │   │       ├── main.rs / cli.rs   # entry point, argument parsing │   │       ├── codegen/          # pure string generators (Containerfile, Quadlet) │   │       ├── commands/         # one module per subcommand Per-file listings rot on every refactor — this one already did — so the tree stops at directories. find crates/ /src -name '.rs' fills in the rest. Pure codegen: codegen:: functions are pure — data in, string out. · X11 companion: when [integration].x11 = true, codegen emits an optional host-side xwayland-satellite unit that connects to the same Wayland socket as native GUI applications and exposes an isolated X socket directory. No I/O, no env reads. · Boundary separation: I/O lives in the thin modules (commands/, build/, quadlet_install/, "},{"kind":"section","title":"Exit Codes","page":"Architecture","path":"/docs/architecture#exit-codes","text":"Code Meaning · 0 Success · 1 General error · 2 Configuration error · 3 Container missing · 4 Build or inspect failure · 5 Missing dependency (podman not found) · 6 Pull or tag failure"},{"kind":"page","title":"Base packages","page":"Base packages","path":"/docs/baked-in-packages","text":"Packages podbox installs into custom-built images automatically — base tools, shells, locales, sudo, and timezone."},{"kind":"section","title":"What's included","page":"Base packages","path":"/docs/baked-in-packages#whats-included","text":"Group Packages · Privilege escalation sudo · Downloading curl, wget · Archives tar, unzip · Locators which · Core tools coreutils, diffutils, findutils, grep, sed, gawk · Shell completion bash-completion (plus zsh-completions on Arch) · Your shell fish, bash, or zsh — whichever $SHELL points at on the host · Locales locales (Debian), glibc-all-langpacks (Fedora), glibc (Arch), musl-locales (Alpine) Host $SHELL Injected · /usr/bin/fish fish · /bin/bash bash, bash-completion · /usr/bin/zsh zsh (+ zsh-completions on Arch, zsh-common on Debian) · /bin/sh, /bin/dash dash Prebuilt images skip this — their shell comes from the image itself."},{"kind":"section","title":"Distro detection","page":"Base packages","path":"/docs/baked-in-packages#distro-detection","text":"The base image name picks the package manager: Family Matched by Manager · Debian debian, ubuntu, mint, kali, pop, elementary apt · Fedora fedora, rhel, centos, rocky, alma, nobara dnf · Arch arch, cachy, manjaro, endeavouros, garuda pacman · Alpine alpine apk · Anything else — dnf Wrong guess? Pin it: [image.packages] manager = \"apk\"   # dnf, apt, pacman, apk, zypper Anything else is ignored and detection applies."},{"kind":"section","title":"Add or remove packages","page":"Base packages","path":"/docs/baked-in-packages#add-or-remove-packages","text":"[image.packages] install = [\"neovim\", \"git\", \"ripgrep\"]   # added on top, duplicates removed remove = [\"vim-minimal\"]                 # strip what the base image ships Leave install empty for a lean image — you still get the base set."},{"kind":"section","title":"Passwordless sudo","page":"Base packages","path":"/docs/baked-in-packages#passwordless-sudo","text":"sudo dnf install … just works, no prompt. The rule regenerates on every start: bet ALL=(ALL) NOPASSWD: ALL"},{"kind":"section","title":"Locale and timezone","page":"Base packages","path":"/docs/baked-in-packages#locale-and-timezone","text":"What How · LANG, LC_ALL, LC_CTYPE Copied from your host environment · /etc/localtime Mounted read-only, if it exists on the host · /etc/timezone Mounted read-only, if it exists (Debian/Ubuntu) Missing files are skipped, not errors. Arch and Fedora don't have /etc/timezone — that's normal."},{"kind":"section","title":"Theme, icon, and font paths","page":"Base packages","path":"/docs/baked-in-packages#theme-icon-and-font-paths","text":"With sync_themes, sync_icons, or sync_fonts on, podbox mounts both the old and new locations (when they exist on the host), so themed apps don't look broken: Old New · /.themes /.local/share/themes · /.icons /.local/share/icons · /.fonts /.local/share/fonts"},{"kind":"page","title":"CLI reference","page":"CLI reference","path":"/docs/cli","text":"podbox CLI reference — every command grouped by workflow, container name resolution, exit codes, JSON output, and shell completion."},{"kind":"section","title":"Command groups","page":"CLI reference","path":"/docs/cli#command-groups","text":"Group Commands · Get started create, init, profile · Day to day enter (alias shell), exec, run, start, stop, restart, list (alias ls), status · Change edit, config get, config set, config validate, build, enable, disable, update, rollback, pull, diff · Inspect logs, inspect, stats, doctor, history, find-definition, info, watch · Copy / backup clone, snapshot, restore, export · Remove remove (alias rm) · Context use · Dotfiles dotfiles sync, dotfiles status · Provision provision sync, provision status · Storage cache list, cache prune serve, compositor, complete-names, and internal-stdin-watchdog are hidden systemd plumbing — callable, but not for daily use."},{"kind":"section","title":"Naming a container","page":"CLI reference","path":"/docs/cli#naming-a-container","text":"Every command resolves its target the same way: positional NAME · -C NAME · $PODBOX_CONTAINER · active context (podbox use) · single config in the config dir / local .podbox.toml A leading name works podman-style too (podbox exec myenv ls), but only for a known config with more arguments after it. Explicit -C always wins. Flag What it does · --here (enter, exec) Start in the container-side twin of your current dir. Only for already-mounted paths — otherwise the error tells you which mount to add. Never sneaks a mount into a running container. · -e KEY=VALUE (repeatable; enter, exec, run) Override env. Host vars cross over only when [container.env].forward names them; explicit -e wins over both. Two settings have no per-command override: network.offline = true is container-wide and persistent. · With [lifecycle].auto_checkpoint = true, update and build --rebuild tag the current image as c"},{"kind":"section","title":"Exit codes","page":"CLI reference","path":"/docs/cli#exit-codes","text":"Code Meaning · Code Meaning · ------ --------- · 0 success · 1 anything else · 2 definition file missing / unreadable: includes find-definition NAME miss, and an explicit --config PATH that does not exist (config_missing) · 3 container/config not found for the requested operation (container_missing) · 4 build failure or podman inspect failure · 5 podman not installed · 6 image pull/tag failure · 64 invalid name: a container, profile or snapshot tag that fails the name rule (invalid_name). Clap argument-parse errors still exit 2 · child's status enter, run and logs exit with the child process's own status; 128+n when it was killed by signal n. No JSON envelope (these take no --output) A missing --config never falls back to a default box. Destructive commands stop before touching anything. Under --dry-run, planned actions print as would: … lines on stderr, so --output json keeps stdout a s"},{"kind":"section","title":"JSON output","page":"CLI reference","path":"/docs/cli#json-output","text":"Read commands take --output json and print nothing else on stdout: list: {\"containers\": [{\"name\",\"status\",\"autostart\",\"active\"}]} · status: {\"name\",\"status\",\"installed\"} · snapshot list: {\"snapshots\": [{\"tag\",\"created\",\"image\"}]} · history: {\"history\": [{\"timestamp\",\"name\",\"action\",\"detail\"}]} Status values: running | stopped | failed | unbuilt. installed says whether Quadlet files exist for an unbuilt container."},{"kind":"section","title":"History","page":"CLI reference","path":"/docs/cli#history","text":"Lifecycle commands log to /.local/state/podbox/history.log on success. Best-effort — logging never fails the command. podbox history              # newest first, all containers (default 25) podbox history myenv        # one container podbox history --limit 0    # no limit; --output json for scripting Recorded: create, build, enable, disable, start, stop, update, remove, recover."},{"kind":"section","title":"Shell completion","page":"CLI reference","path":"/docs/cli#shell-completion","text":"podbox completions bash > ~/.local/share/bash-completion/completions/podbox podbox completions zsh  > \"${fpath[1]}/_podbox\" podbox completions fish > ~/.config/fish/completions/podbox.fish Names complete after name-taking subcommands and as -C values, for bash, zsh, and fish. No configs, no candidates — completion never errors. podbox completions fish --abbrs appends opt-in abbr shorthand (fish only — ignored for bash/zsh): podbox completions fish --abbrs | source Token Expands to  Token Expands to · pb podbox  pbs podbox start · pbb podbox build  pbt podbox stop · pbc podbox create  pbu podbox update · pbd podbox doctor  pbv podbox status · pbe podbox enter  pbx podbox exec -- · pbl podbox list  pbr podbox recover"},{"kind":"command","title":"podbox create","page":"CLI reference","path":"/docs/cli#command-groups","text":"Get started"},{"kind":"command","title":"podbox init","page":"CLI reference","path":"/docs/cli#command-groups","text":"Get started"},{"kind":"command","title":"podbox profile","page":"CLI reference","path":"/docs/cli#command-groups","text":"Get started"},{"kind":"command","title":"podbox enter","page":"CLI reference","path":"/docs/cli#command-groups","text":"Day to day"},{"kind":"command","title":"podbox shell","page":"CLI reference","path":"/docs/cli#command-groups","text":"Day to day"},{"kind":"command","title":"podbox exec","page":"CLI reference","path":"/docs/cli#command-groups","text":"Day to day"},{"kind":"command","title":"podbox run","page":"CLI reference","path":"/docs/cli#command-groups","text":"Day to day"},{"kind":"command","title":"podbox start","page":"CLI reference","path":"/docs/cli#command-groups","text":"Day to day"},{"kind":"command","title":"podbox stop","page":"CLI reference","path":"/docs/cli#command-groups","text":"Day to day"},{"kind":"command","title":"podbox restart","page":"CLI reference","path":"/docs/cli#command-groups","text":"Day to day"},{"kind":"command","title":"podbox list","page":"CLI reference","path":"/docs/cli#command-groups","text":"Day to day"},{"kind":"command","title":"podbox ls","page":"CLI reference","path":"/docs/cli#command-groups","text":"Day to day"},{"kind":"command","title":"podbox status","page":"CLI reference","path":"/docs/cli#command-groups","text":"Day to day"},{"kind":"command","title":"podbox edit","page":"CLI reference","path":"/docs/cli#command-groups","text":"Change"},{"kind":"command","title":"podbox config get","page":"CLI reference","path":"/docs/cli#command-groups","text":"Change"},{"kind":"command","title":"podbox config set","page":"CLI reference","path":"/docs/cli#command-groups","text":"Change"},{"kind":"command","title":"podbox config validate","page":"CLI reference","path":"/docs/cli#command-groups","text":"Change"},{"kind":"command","title":"podbox build","page":"CLI reference","path":"/docs/cli#command-groups","text":"Change"},{"kind":"command","title":"podbox enable","page":"CLI reference","path":"/docs/cli#command-groups","text":"Change"},{"kind":"command","title":"podbox disable","page":"CLI reference","path":"/docs/cli#command-groups","text":"Change"},{"kind":"command","title":"podbox update","page":"CLI reference","path":"/docs/cli#command-groups","text":"Change"},{"kind":"command","title":"podbox rollback","page":"CLI reference","path":"/docs/cli#command-groups","text":"Change"},{"kind":"command","title":"podbox pull","page":"CLI reference","path":"/docs/cli#command-groups","text":"Change"},{"kind":"command","title":"podbox diff","page":"CLI reference","path":"/docs/cli#command-groups","text":"Change"},{"kind":"command","title":"podbox logs","page":"CLI reference","path":"/docs/cli#command-groups","text":"Inspect"},{"kind":"command","title":"podbox inspect","page":"CLI reference","path":"/docs/cli#command-groups","text":"Inspect"},{"kind":"command","title":"podbox stats","page":"CLI reference","path":"/docs/cli#command-groups","text":"Inspect"},{"kind":"command","title":"podbox doctor","page":"CLI reference","path":"/docs/cli#command-groups","text":"Inspect"},{"kind":"command","title":"podbox history","page":"CLI reference","path":"/docs/cli#command-groups","text":"Inspect"},{"kind":"command","title":"podbox find-definition","page":"CLI reference","path":"/docs/cli#command-groups","text":"Inspect"},{"kind":"command","title":"podbox info","page":"CLI reference","path":"/docs/cli#command-groups","text":"Inspect"},{"kind":"command","title":"podbox watch","page":"CLI reference","path":"/docs/cli#command-groups","text":"Inspect"},{"kind":"command","title":"podbox clone","page":"CLI reference","path":"/docs/cli#command-groups","text":"Copy / backup"},{"kind":"command","title":"podbox snapshot","page":"CLI reference","path":"/docs/cli#command-groups","text":"Copy / backup"},{"kind":"command","title":"podbox restore","page":"CLI reference","path":"/docs/cli#command-groups","text":"Copy / backup"},{"kind":"command","title":"podbox export","page":"CLI reference","path":"/docs/cli#command-groups","text":"Copy / backup"},{"kind":"command","title":"podbox remove","page":"CLI reference","path":"/docs/cli#command-groups","text":"Remove"},{"kind":"command","title":"podbox rm","page":"CLI reference","path":"/docs/cli#command-groups","text":"Remove"},{"kind":"command","title":"podbox use","page":"CLI reference","path":"/docs/cli#command-groups","text":"Context"},{"kind":"command","title":"podbox dotfiles sync","page":"CLI reference","path":"/docs/cli#command-groups","text":"Dotfiles"},{"kind":"command","title":"podbox dotfiles status","page":"CLI reference","path":"/docs/cli#command-groups","text":"Dotfiles"},{"kind":"command","title":"podbox provision sync","page":"CLI reference","path":"/docs/cli#command-groups","text":"Provision"},{"kind":"command","title":"podbox provision status","page":"CLI reference","path":"/docs/cli#command-groups","text":"Provision"},{"kind":"command","title":"podbox cache list","page":"CLI reference","path":"/docs/cli#command-groups","text":"Storage"},{"kind":"command","title":"podbox cache prune","page":"CLI reference","path":"/docs/cli#command-groups","text":"Storage"},{"kind":"page","title":"Building clients","page":"Building clients","path":"/docs/clients","text":"Build clients on podbox — the CLI as a stable contract: name handling, JSON output, errors, exit codes, streaming, and what is proposed next."},{"kind":"section","title":"Ground rules","page":"Building clients","path":"/docs/clients#ground-rules","text":"Always pass the name. Never depend on the active context (podbox use). Use a positional NAME or -C NAME. Today · Use argv arrays, never a shell string. Spawn podbox directly with an argument list. No sh -c, no string interpolation. Today · Read JSON, not text. Use --output json wherever it exists. Only JSON output is covered by the stability promise. Today · Treat unknown fields as ignorable and known-optional fields as optional. Only the fields listed as required below are guaranteed. Today · Check the exit code first. Then parse stdout. Today · Preview with --dry-run. Every command accepts it. Today"},{"kind":"section","title":"Name resolution","page":"Building clients","path":"/docs/clients#name-resolution","text":"Every command resolves its target in the same order: positional NAME · -C NAME · $PODBOX_CONTAINER · active context · the single config in the config directory, or a local .podbox.toml Clients should use 1 or 2. Two namespaces: the config file stem is the lookup key (-C, use, completion); container.name is the runtime name shown by list and status and used for units, containers, and exports. They match when the config is created normally and diverge only if hand-edited — clients should always display the runtime name. Proposed: one documented name rule, ^[A-Za-z0-9_.-]+$, enforced by podbox itself so clients can validate against the same pattern."},{"kind":"section","title":"Reading state","page":"Building clients","path":"/docs/clients#reading-state","text":"All of these print only JSON on stdout when --output json is given."},{"kind":"section","title":"podbox list --output json **Today**","page":"Building clients","path":"/docs/clients#podbox-list---output-json-today","text":"{\"schema\": 1, \"containers\": [{\"name\": \"myenv\", \"status\": \"running\", \"autostart\": true, \"active\": false}]} Field Required Notes · name yes Environment name. · status yes running, stopped, failed or unbuilt. · autostart no Whether the unit starts on login. · active no Whether this is the active context."},{"kind":"section","title":"podbox status NAME --output json **Today**","page":"Building clients","path":"/docs/clients#podbox-status-name---output-json-today","text":"{\"schema\": 1, \"name\": \"myenv\", \"status\": \"running\", \"installed\": true} installed says whether Quadlet files exist, which matters for unbuilt environments. Optional enrichment, so a client needs one call, not four. Today Each field is present when readable, absent otherwise: Field Meaning · base_image The image the environment is built from, or the prebuilt reference. · started_at Last-start timestamp as reported by podman inspect. · cpu_percent Current CPU use (running containers only). · memory_bytes Current memory use (running containers only). · memory_limit_bytes Configured limit, or absent. · pids Number of processes (running containers only)."},{"kind":"section","title":"Other readers **Today**","page":"Building clients","path":"/docs/clients#other-readers-today","text":"Command Output · podbox stats NAME --no-stream --output json Resource snapshot. Field names are not yet documented. · podbox history [NAME] --output json {\"history\": [{\"timestamp\",\"name\",\"action\",\"detail\"}]} · podbox snapshot list NAME --output json {\"snapshots\": [{\"tag\",\"created\",\"image\"}]} · podbox provision status NAME --output json {\"steps\": [{\"name\",\"state\",\"applied_at\"}]} · podbox inspect NAME --toml The resolved config. !!! note \"\" stats is the least stable reader today. Document its shape before clients build on it."},{"kind":"section","title":"Acting on an environment","page":"Building clients","path":"/docs/clients#acting-on-an-environment","text":"Action Command Status · Start podbox start NAME Today · Stop podbox stop NAME Today · Restart podbox restart NAME Today · Enter a shell podbox enter NAME (starts it if needed) Today · Run a command podbox exec NAME -- CMD Today · Open the config podbox edit NAME Today · Build podbox build NAME Today · Remove podbox remove NAME Today Removal runs on definitions that fail validation (a validation error is demoted to a warning); only structural breaks — unknown keys, a newer schema — refuse, because guessing around those is not safe for a destructive verb."},{"kind":"section","title":"Interactive commands","page":"Building clients","path":"/docs/clients#interactive-commands","text":"enter, exec, edit, logs -f and init -i need a terminal. A GUI client should not embed one. Launch the user's own terminal and run the command in it."},{"kind":"section","title":"Mutating commands in JSON mode","page":"Building clients","path":"/docs/clients#mutating-commands-in-json-mode","text":"Proposed: start, stop, create, build and export accept --output json and print the resulting state: {\"ok\": true, \"name\": \"myenv\", \"status\": \"running\"} They never prompt when --output json or --yes is given."},{"kind":"section","title":"Exports","page":"Building clients","path":"/docs/clients#exports","text":"Action Command Status · Export an app podbox export app NAME ITEM Today · Export a command podbox export bin NAME ITEM Today · Remove everything exported podbox export clean with -C NAME Today · List exportable items and what is exported podbox export list NAME --output json Today · Remove one item podbox export remove app|bin ITEM with -C NAME Today App export reads the .desktop file with podman exec, so the environment must be running. export list reports the union of declared ([integration.export]) items and what is actually on the host: {\"schema\": 1,  \"apps\": [{\"id\": \"gedit\", \"name\": \"Text Editor (myenv)\", \"exported\": true}],  \"bins\": [{\"name\": \"rg\", \"path\": \"/usr/bin/rg\", \"exported\": true}]} name is the desktop file's display name; path is the in-container absolute path. Both are absent when they cannot be resolved (missing export, stopped box), while exported tells the two states a"},{"kind":"section","title":"Creating and configuring","page":"Building clients","path":"/docs/clients#creating-and-configuring","text":"podbox create PROFILE --name NAME --yes never prompts. Today Without --name, --yes derives the container name from a raw image reference instead of stopping with a suggestion. podbox config get KEY prints one effective value (defaults and extends applied): scalars print raw, tables and arrays print as JSON. Today podbox config set KEY VALUE edits the file with toml_edit, so comments and formatting survive. Today Values parse as TOML (4, true, [\"a\"]); anything else becomes a string. The edit is verified before it lands — unknown keys and invalid values fail without touching the file. Setting a key under image. reports that a rebuild is needed. Array indices (provision[0]) are not addressable yet. podbox config validate --output json returns the verdict with line numbers. Today {\"schema\": 1, \"valid\": false,  \"errors\": [{\"field\": \"container.memory\", \"line\": 12, \"col\": null,              \"me"},{"kind":"section","title":"Errors and exit codes","page":"Building clients","path":"/docs/clients#errors-and-exit-codes","text":"Exit codes are part of the contract. Today Code Meaning · Code Meaning · ------ --------- · 0 Success · 1 Any other error · 2 Definition file missing or unreadable, including an explicit --config path that does not exist (config_missing) · 3 Container or config not found (container_missing) · 4 Build failure or inspect failure · 5 podman not installed · 6 Image pull or tag failure · 64 Invalid name: a container, profile or snapshot tag that fails the name rule (invalid_name). Clap argument-parse errors still exit 2 · child's status enter, run, logs: the child's own exit status, or 128+n for signal n (child_exit) child_exit has no fixed number: it is whatever the child exited with. These commands take no --output, so there is no JSON envelope for it. Read the exit status. Data goes to stdout. Diagnostics go to stderr. A client should show stderr to the user, and should not parse it. In JS"},{"kind":"section","title":"Staying up to date without polling","page":"Building clients","path":"/docs/clients#staying-up-to-date-without-polling","text":"A client that wants live state has two options. Poll list. Every 5 to 10 seconds is enough for a status indicator. Never run two overlapping polls of the same command. Today · Subscribe: podbox watch. Today Runs in the foreground and prints the current state once, then one compact JSON line per change: json {\"schema\": 1, \"event\": \"status\", \"name\": \"myenv\", \"status\": \"running\"} The client spawns it as a child process and stops it when it is done. Nothing runs in the background, so the no-daemon promise holds. Filter with a positional NAME, pace with --interval SECONDS (default 2), render human transitions with --output text. --dry-run prints the poll plan. Builds can be slow. podbox build --output ndjson prints progress events, ending with a done line. Today (--dry-run always prints the human plan): {\"schema\": 1, \"event\": \"step\", \"index\": 2, \"total\": 3, \"text\": \"Building image ...\"} {\"sch"},{"kind":"section","title":"Version and capability checks","page":"Building clients","path":"/docs/clients#version-and-capability-checks","text":"podbox info --output json needs no config and never fails for a missing box. Today {\"schema\": 1, \"podbox\": \"0.8.2\", \"podman\": \"5.6.0\", \"features\": [\"export_list\", \"restart\"]} podman is null when podman is not installed. features is additive: probe for the flags you need, tolerate unknown ones. New fields and new commands never bump schema; only a breaking change does. Every podbox-owned JSON payload carries \"schema\": 1. Clients should refuse to run against a higher schema than they know. Exception: stats --output json is a raw podman stats passthrough with no envelope. It prints podman's own JSON array (one object per container, all values strings — CPU like \"0.12%\", MemUsage like \"12.3MiB / 2GiB\", PIDs), whose exact fields follow the installed podman version. Treat it as informational, not contractual."},{"kind":"section","title":"Safety","page":"Building clients","path":"/docs/clients#safety","text":"Validate environment and item names before passing them to any command. · Treat all text from podbox and from container files as untrusted. Do not render it as markup. · Time out reads after a few seconds and show a quiet error instead of freezing the interface. · If podbox is not installed (exit code 5, or not found on PATH), do not retry on every poll. Check again when the user opens the client."},{"kind":"section","title":"Testing against the contract","page":"Building clients","path":"/docs/clients#testing-against-the-contract","text":"podbox keeps golden JSON fixtures in crates/podbox/tests/fixtures/golden/ for every sandbox-runnable reader above, and cargo test -p podbox-cli --test golden fails when an output shape changes. (diff is excluded: it needs a running container.) Clients can copy these fixtures to build their own tests without a running container. Today"},{"kind":"section","title":"Checklist for maintainers","page":"Building clients","path":"/docs/clients#checklist-for-maintainers","text":"In priority order: Document this page's Today items and the stats shape. Page written; stats documented as passthrough. · Extend status --output json with the optional fields. Done. · Add export list --output json and per-item removal. Done. · Add restart. Done. · Add \"schema\": 1 to every JSON payload and the structured error format. Done. · Add info, config get|set|validate, and non-interactive create. Done. · Add watch and build --output ndjson. Done. · Add the golden fixtures. Done."},{"kind":"key","title":"image.base","page":"Configuration Reference","path":"/docs/config#image","text":"string · required · Base container image (e.g. \"fedora:44\")"},{"kind":"key","title":"image.name","page":"Configuration Reference","path":"/docs/config#image","text":"string · required · Image tag name (e.g. \"myenv\")"},{"kind":"key","title":"image.image","page":"Configuration Reference","path":"/docs/config#image","text":"string · — · Prebuilt image reference (e.g. \"ghcr.io/user/myenv:latest\"). When set, podbox uses the registry image instead of building from base"},{"kind":"key","title":"image.pull_retry","page":"Configuration Reference","path":"/docs/config#image","text":"int · 3 · Number of pull retries on failure"},{"kind":"key","title":"image.pull_retry_delay","page":"Configuration Reference","path":"/docs/config#image","text":"string · \"5s\" · Delay between pull retries"},{"kind":"key","title":"image.packages.install","page":"Configuration Reference","path":"/docs/config#imagepackages","text":"string[] · [] · Packages to install via the distro package manager"},{"kind":"key","title":"image.packages.remove","page":"Configuration Reference","path":"/docs/config#imagepackages","text":"string[] · [] · Packages to remove"},{"kind":"key","title":"image.packages.manager","page":"Configuration Reference","path":"/docs/config#imagepackages","text":"string · auto-detected · Package manager override: dnf, apt, pacman, apk, zypper. Auto-detected from the base image name when omitted"},{"kind":"key","title":"image.run.commands","page":"Configuration Reference","path":"/docs/config#imagerun","text":"string[] · [] · Extra RUN commands in the Containerfile"},{"kind":"key","title":"container.name","page":"Configuration Reference","path":"/docs/config#container","text":"string · required · Container name (used for systemd unit names, socket paths)"},{"kind":"key","title":"container.home","page":"Configuration Reference","path":"/docs/config#container","text":"string · required · Host path for isolated home ( expands)"},{"kind":"key","title":"container.shell","page":"Configuration Reference","path":"/docs/config#container","text":"string · \"bash\" · Default login shell inside the container"},{"kind":"key","title":"container.memory","page":"Configuration Reference","path":"/docs/config#container","text":"string · — · Memory limit (e.g. \"4G\", \"2048M\"). Passed as Memory= in Quadlet"},{"kind":"key","title":"container.cpus","page":"Configuration Reference","path":"/docs/config#container","text":"string · — · CPU limit (e.g. \"2.0\", \"0.5\"). Passed through to Podman as --cpus"},{"kind":"key","title":"container.slice","page":"Configuration Reference","path":"/docs/config#container","text":"string · \"podbox.slice\" · systemd slice for the container service"},{"kind":"key","title":"container.cpu_weight","page":"Configuration Reference","path":"/docs/config#container","text":"integer · 200 · systemd CPU contention weight (1–10000)"},{"kind":"key","title":"container.reload_cmd","page":"Configuration Reference","path":"/docs/config#container","text":"string · — · Command run on config reload. Passed as ReloadCmd= in Quadlet"},{"kind":"key","title":"container.mounts.extra","page":"Configuration Reference","path":"/docs/config#containermounts","text":"string[] · [] · Extra Volume= lines (e.g. \"/Work:/home/user/Work:z\")"},{"kind":"key","title":"container.env.forward","page":"Configuration Reference","path":"/docs/config#containerenv","text":"string[] · [] · Host variables to forward for enter, exec, and run; supports exact names and PREFIX patterns"},{"kind":"key","title":"security.apparmor","page":"Configuration Reference","path":"/docs/config#security","text":"string · — · AppArmor profile name. Passed as AppArmor= in Quadlet (\"unconfined\" to disable)"},{"kind":"key","title":"security.seccomp","page":"Configuration Reference","path":"/docs/config#security","text":"string · — · Seccomp profile path, \"default\", or \"unconfined\". Passed as SeccompProfile="},{"kind":"key","title":"security.security_label_disable","page":"Configuration Reference","path":"/docs/config#security","text":"bool · true · Disable SELinux process labeling. Emits SecurityLabelDisable=true when set"},{"kind":"key","title":"security.no_new_privileges","page":"Configuration Reference","path":"/docs/config#security","text":"bool · true · Block privilege escalation via setuid binaries (sudo, su, AUR helpers). Emits NoNewPrivileges=true in the Quadlet. Set false to allow."},{"kind":"key","title":"security.read_only_rootfs","page":"Configuration Reference","path":"/docs/config#security","text":"bool · false · Make root filesystem read-only. Emits ReadOnly=true in Quadlet"},{"kind":"key","title":"security.userns","page":"Configuration Reference","path":"/docs/config#security","text":"string · — · User namespace mode override. Defaults to \"keep-id\". Supported: \"keep-id\", \"nomap\", \"private\""},{"kind":"key","title":"security.cap_preset","page":"Configuration Reference","path":"/docs/config#security","text":"string · \"default\" · Capability preset. Options: \"none\", \"default\", \"monitoring\", \"admin\". Adds a predefined set of --cap-add entries alongside any cap_add list below"},{"kind":"key","title":"security.cap_add","page":"Configuration Reference","path":"/docs/config#security","text":"string[] · [] · Extra Linux capabilities to add (e.g. [\"SYS_ADMIN\"]). Combined with cap_preset caps"},{"kind":"key","title":"security.secrets","page":"Configuration Reference","path":"/docs/config#security","text":"table[] · [] · Secrets passed to the container without baking them into the image (see below)"},{"kind":"key","title":"security.secrets.name","page":"Configuration Reference","path":"/docs/config#securitysecrets","text":"string · required · Secret name to read"},{"kind":"key","title":"security.secrets.type","page":"Configuration Reference","path":"/docs/config#securitysecrets","text":"string · \"env\" · env (environment variable) or mount (file in the container)"},{"kind":"key","title":"security.secrets.target","page":"Configuration Reference","path":"/docs/config#securitysecrets","text":"string · secret name · Destination name inside the container"},{"kind":"key","title":"security.secrets.mode","page":"Configuration Reference","path":"/docs/config#securitysecrets","text":"string · — · File mode for mount secrets"},{"kind":"key","title":"security.secrets.source","page":"Configuration Reference","path":"/docs/config#securitysecrets","text":"string · \"podman\" · podman reads podman secret; systemd reads a systemd credential"},{"kind":"key","title":"security.secrets.path","page":"Configuration Reference","path":"/docs/config#securitysecrets","text":"string · — · Host-side credential file. Required with source = \"systemd\""},{"kind":"key","title":"security.secrets.encrypted","page":"Configuration Reference","path":"/docs/config#securitysecrets","text":"bool · false · Read path with LoadCredentialEncrypted= instead of LoadCredential=. Only meaningful with source = \"systemd\""},{"kind":"key","title":"network.mode","page":"Configuration Reference","path":"/docs/config#network","text":"string · \"pasta\" · Network mode: \"host\", \"bridge\", \"none\", \"pasta\", \"slirp4netns\", \"private\". Defaults to \"pasta\" (user-space NAT with working networking). \"private\" is loopback only — no host sockets or localhost services. \"host\" shares the host network — choose it deliberately"},{"kind":"key","title":"network.ports","page":"Configuration Reference","path":"/docs/config#network","text":"string[] · [] · Port mappings (\"hostPort:containerPort\"). Emitted as PublishPort= in Quadlet (ignored in host mode)"},{"kind":"key","title":"storage.shared_caches.cargo","page":"Configuration Reference","path":"/docs/config#storageshared_caches","text":"bool · false · /.cargo/registry, /.cargo/git"},{"kind":"key","title":"storage.shared_caches.npm","page":"Configuration Reference","path":"/docs/config#storageshared_caches","text":"bool · false · /.npm"},{"kind":"key","title":"storage.shared_caches.pnpm","page":"Configuration Reference","path":"/docs/config#storageshared_caches","text":"bool · false · /.local/share/pnpm/store"},{"kind":"key","title":"storage.shared_caches.pip","page":"Configuration Reference","path":"/docs/config#storageshared_caches","text":"bool · false · /.cache/pip"},{"kind":"key","title":"storage.shared_caches.uv","page":"Configuration Reference","path":"/docs/config#storageshared_caches","text":"bool · false · /.cache/uv"},{"kind":"key","title":"storage.shared_caches.yarn","page":"Configuration Reference","path":"/docs/config#storageshared_caches","text":"bool · false · /.cache/yarn, /.yarn/berry/cache"},{"kind":"key","title":"storage.shared_caches.bun","page":"Configuration Reference","path":"/docs/config#storageshared_caches","text":"bool · false · /.bun/install/cache"},{"kind":"key","title":"storage.shared_caches.composer","page":"Configuration Reference","path":"/docs/config#storageshared_caches","text":"bool · false · /.cache/composer"},{"kind":"key","title":"storage.shared_caches.maven","page":"Configuration Reference","path":"/docs/config#storageshared_caches","text":"bool · false · /.m2/repository"},{"kind":"key","title":"storage.shared_caches.gradle","page":"Configuration Reference","path":"/docs/config#storageshared_caches","text":"bool · false · /.gradle/caches"},{"kind":"key","title":"storage.shared_caches.ccache","page":"Configuration Reference","path":"/docs/config#storageshared_caches","text":"bool · false · /.cache/ccache"},{"kind":"key","title":"storage.shared_caches.go","page":"Configuration Reference","path":"/docs/config#storageshared_caches","text":"bool · false · /go/pkg/mod"},{"kind":"key","title":"storage.shared_caches.rustup","page":"Configuration Reference","path":"/docs/config#storageshared_caches","text":"bool · false · /.rustup"},{"kind":"key","title":"storage.shared_caches.mbx","page":"Configuration Reference","path":"/docs/config#storageshared_caches","text":"bool · false · /.cache/mbx"},{"kind":"key","title":"storage.shared_caches.custom[].name","page":"Configuration Reference","path":"/docs/config#storageshared_caches","text":"string · — · Built-in names are reserved"},{"kind":"key","title":"storage.shared_caches.custom[].container_path","page":"Configuration Reference","path":"/docs/config#storageshared_caches","text":"string · — · Destination in the container, /… or absolute"},{"kind":"key","title":"storage.host_caches.cargo","page":"Configuration Reference","path":"/docs/config#storagehost_caches","text":"bool · false · /.cargo/registry and /.cargo/git"},{"kind":"key","title":"storage.host_caches.npm, pnpm, pip, uv, yarn, bun, composer, maven, gradle, ccache, go, rustup, mbx","page":"Configuration Reference","path":"/docs/config#storagehost_caches","text":"bool · false · That tool's cache; see the table above for paths"},{"kind":"key","title":"storage.host_caches.custom[].name","page":"Configuration Reference","path":"/docs/config#storagehost_caches","text":"string · — · Label used in error messages; built-in names are reserved"},{"kind":"key","title":"storage.host_caches.custom[].host_path","page":"Configuration Reference","path":"/docs/config#storagehost_caches","text":"string · — · Path on the host, /… or absolute"},{"kind":"key","title":"storage.host_caches.custom[].container_path","page":"Configuration Reference","path":"/docs/config#storagehost_caches","text":"string · — · Destination in the container, /… or absolute"},{"kind":"key","title":"integration.wayland","page":"Configuration Reference","path":"/docs/config#integration","text":"bool · false · Share Wayland socket for GUI apps"},{"kind":"key","title":"integration.x11","page":"Configuration Reference","path":"/docs/config#integration","text":"bool · false · Run X11 apps through a per-container xwayland-satellite bridge; requires wayland = true"},{"kind":"key","title":"integration.audio","page":"Configuration Reference","path":"/docs/config#integration","text":"bool · false · Share PipeWire/PulseAudio sockets"},{"kind":"key","title":"integration.gpu","page":"Configuration Reference","path":"/docs/config#integration","text":"string/bool · \"auto\" · GPU passthrough (true, false, \"auto\", \"nvidia\")"},{"kind":"key","title":"integration.dbus","page":"Configuration Reference","path":"/docs/config#integration","text":"bool · false · Enable D-Bus session bus access. Talk/own/preset rules without it are rejected; a rules set without the switch stated (a pre-0.9.0 file, where the bus defaulted on) revives it with a notice"},{"kind":"key","title":"integration.notify","page":"Configuration Reference","path":"/docs/config#integration","text":"bool · false · Desktop notification forwarding"},{"kind":"key","title":"integration.xdg_open","page":"Configuration Reference","path":"/docs/config#integration","text":"bool or table · false · URI opening via host (xdg-open). The bool form uses the default allowlist (http, https, mailto); the table form is { enabled = bool, allowed_schemes = [string, ...] }, and naming schemes implies enabled = true. An empty stored list means the default list. Schemes must be RFC 3986 lowercase scheme tokens (letters, digits, +, -, .; e.g. smb, mailto, steam); invalid tokens are a parse error. Widening this list lets container-supplied URIs reach host handlers for those schemes (file managers, editors, mail clients) — add only schemes you understand; see guest.md"},{"kind":"key","title":"integration.clipboard","page":"Configuration Reference","path":"/docs/config#integration","text":"bool · false · Clipboard sharing"},{"kind":"key","title":"integration.sync_fonts","page":"Configuration Reference","path":"/docs/config#integration","text":"bool · false · Bind-mount /.fonts and /.local/share/fonts (read-only) when present on the host"},{"kind":"key","title":"integration.sync_icons","page":"Configuration Reference","path":"/docs/config#integration","text":"bool · false · Bind-mount /.icons and /.local/share/icons (read-only) when present on the host"},{"kind":"key","title":"integration.sync_themes","page":"Configuration Reference","path":"/docs/config#integration","text":"bool · false · Bind-mount /.themes and /.local/share/themes (read-only) when present on the host"},{"kind":"key","title":"integration.gpg_agent","page":"Configuration Reference","path":"/docs/config#integration","text":"bool · false · Forward GPG agent socket (S.gpg-agent). Sets GPG_TTY and GNUPGHOME"},{"kind":"key","title":"integration.git_identity","page":"Configuration Reference","path":"/docs/config#integration","text":"bool · false · Bridge host Git identity and safe.directory into the container. Needs git in the image — podbox never installs it, so this is a no-op without it"},{"kind":"key","title":"integration.host_exec","page":"Configuration Reference","path":"/docs/config#integration","text":"table · { enabled = false } · Host command execution (see [[integration.host_exec]](#integrationhost_exec) below)"},{"kind":"key","title":"integration.ssh_agent","page":"Configuration Reference","path":"/docs/config#integration","text":"bool · false · Forward SSH agent socket ($SSH_AUTH_SOCK). Requires Podman ≥ 5.6"},{"kind":"key","title":"integration.hardware.kvm","page":"Configuration Reference","path":"/docs/config#integrationhardware","text":"bool · false · /dev/kvm — nested virtualisation, Android emulators"},{"kind":"key","title":"integration.hardware.joystick","page":"Configuration Reference","path":"/docs/config#integrationhardware","text":"bool · false · /dev/input, /dev/uinput — gamepads and joysticks"},{"kind":"key","title":"integration.hardware.webcam","page":"Configuration Reference","path":"/docs/config#integrationhardware","text":"bool · false · /dev/video, /dev/media"},{"kind":"key","title":"integration.hardware.serial","page":"Configuration Reference","path":"/docs/config#integrationhardware","text":"bool · false · /dev/ttyUSB, /dev/ttyACM — microcontrollers"},{"kind":"key","title":"integration.hardware.yubikey","page":"Configuration Reference","path":"/docs/config#integrationhardware","text":"bool · false · pcscd socket and /dev/hidraw — smartcards, 2FA"},{"kind":"key","title":"integration.hardware.usb","page":"Configuration Reference","path":"/docs/config#integrationhardware","text":"bool · false · /dev/bus/usb (bind-mounted, hotplug-aware) — adb, fastboot, flashers, SDR"},{"kind":"key","title":"integration.host_exec.enabled","page":"Configuration Reference","path":"/docs/config#integrationhost_exec","text":"bool · false · Allow container to execute commands on the host"},{"kind":"key","title":"integration.host_exec.allowlist","page":"Configuration Reference","path":"/docs/config#integrationhost_exec","text":"table · (none) · Allowed commands as alias → path pairs. When set, only those commands may run; when absent, anything may run. Each value may also be a detailed table { path = \"/usr/bin/…\", filter = true, shim = true, timeout = 30 }"},{"kind":"key","title":"integration.host_exec.cwd","page":"Configuration Reference","path":"/docs/config#integrationhost_exec","text":"string · \"home\" · What the fd-passing session does with the guest working directory (see cwd handling below)"},{"kind":"key","title":"integration.xdg_dirs.documents","page":"Configuration Reference","path":"/docs/config#integrationxdg_dirs","text":"bool · false · Mount host /Documents"},{"kind":"key","title":"integration.xdg_dirs.downloads","page":"Configuration Reference","path":"/docs/config#integrationxdg_dirs","text":"bool · false · Mount host /Downloads"},{"kind":"key","title":"integration.xdg_dirs.pictures","page":"Configuration Reference","path":"/docs/config#integrationxdg_dirs","text":"bool · false · Mount host /Pictures"},{"kind":"key","title":"integration.xdg_dirs.music","page":"Configuration Reference","path":"/docs/config#integrationxdg_dirs","text":"bool · false · Mount host /Music"},{"kind":"key","title":"integration.xdg_dirs.videos","page":"Configuration Reference","path":"/docs/config#integrationxdg_dirs","text":"bool · false · Mount host /Videos"},{"kind":"key","title":"integration.xdg_dirs.desktop","page":"Configuration Reference","path":"/docs/config#integrationxdg_dirs","text":"bool · false · Mount host /Desktop"},{"kind":"key","title":"integration.xdg_dirs.projects","page":"Configuration Reference","path":"/docs/config#integrationxdg_dirs","text":"bool · false · Mount host /Projects"},{"kind":"key","title":"integration.export.apps","page":"Configuration Reference","path":"/docs/config#integrationexport","text":"string[] · [] · App .desktop files to export (without .desktop suffix)"},{"kind":"key","title":"integration.export.bins","page":"Configuration Reference","path":"/docs/config#integrationexport","text":"string[] · [] · Binary shims to generate in /.local/bin"},{"kind":"key","title":"lifecycle.quadlet","page":"Configuration Reference","path":"/docs/config#lifecycle","text":"bool · false · Generate Quadlet systemd files on podbox enable"},{"kind":"key","title":"lifecycle.autostart","page":"Configuration Reference","path":"/docs/config#lifecycle","text":"bool · false · Start container on user login (WantedBy=default.target)"},{"kind":"key","title":"lifecycle.on_stop","page":"Configuration Reference","path":"/docs/config#lifecycle","text":"string · \"keep\" · Deprecated, no effect: Quadlet containers are always ephemeral (podman run --rm --replace); state lives in the home volume and snapshots. Setting it prints a deprecation warning"},{"kind":"key","title":"lifecycle.auto_update","page":"Configuration Reference","path":"/docs/config#lifecycle","text":"bool · false · Add Label=io.containers.autoupdate=registry for auto-updates"},{"kind":"key","title":"lifecycle.auto_checkpoint","page":"Configuration Reference","path":"/docs/config#lifecycle","text":"bool · false · Tag the current image as checkpoint-prev before update or build --rebuild; podbox rollback restores that image"},{"kind":"key","title":"lifecycle.idle_timeout","page":"Configuration Reference","path":"/docs/config#lifecycle","text":"string · \"off\" · Idle timeout before guest daemon exits (\"off\", \"30s\", \"5m\", \"1h\")"},{"kind":"key","title":"systemd.requires","page":"Configuration Reference","path":"/docs/config#systemd","text":"string[] · [] · Units that must be active before the container (Requires=)"},{"kind":"key","title":"systemd.after","page":"Configuration Reference","path":"/docs/config#systemd","text":"string[] · [] · Units the container should start after (After=)"},{"kind":"key","title":"dbus.preset","page":"Configuration Reference","path":"/docs/config#dbus","text":"string · \"none\" · Preset filling talk for you: \"flatpak\", \"gnome\", \"kde\", \"portal\" (adds org.freedesktop.Notifications talk). Any preset other than \"none\" implies the proxy — unknown values are a parse error. Portal names are never granted via talk — they come through interface-scoped rules for notify / xdg_open (see dbus-proxy.md)"},{"kind":"key","title":"dbus.talk","page":"Configuration Reference","path":"/docs/config#dbus","text":"string[] · [] · D-Bus services the container can call (two-way). Adding a portal-family name re-grants the full portal surface — a warning is printed"},{"kind":"key","title":"dbus.own","page":"Configuration Reference","path":"/docs/config#dbus","text":"string[] · [] · D-Bus services the container can register on the host bus"},{"kind":"key","title":"wayland.firewall","page":"Configuration Reference","path":"/docs/config#wayland","text":"bool · true · Filter Wayland protocol access through the compositor proxy"},{"kind":"key","title":"wayland.blocked_interfaces","page":"Configuration Reference","path":"/docs/config#wayland","text":"string[] · (see below) · Wayland globals to deny. Replaces the default list when set"},{"kind":"page","title":"Configuration Reference","page":"Configuration Reference","path":"/docs/config","text":"Complete TOML configuration reference for podbox — all keys, defaults, and examples for image, container, integration, lifecycle, and D-Bus settings."},{"kind":"section","title":"Inheritance (extends)","page":"Configuration Reference","path":"/docs/config#inheritance-extends","text":"A definition may start from another file with extends: a path (./base.toml), a profile (profile:dev, or builtin:dev for the shipped template even when you shadow it), or a sibling name. The parent merges first, then the child overrides. Tables merge key by key; scalars are overwritten by the child. Arrays merge by kind: Set-like lists (packages, ports, mounts, blocked interfaces, …) union with deduplication, parent order first. · Prefix an entry with ! to remove a parent entry instead: install = [\"!nano\", \"vim\"] drops the parent's nano and adds vim. · [image.run].commands appends verbatim — order and repetition matter for RUN steps, so nothing is deduplicated there. · [[provision]] steps merge by name: a child step with the same name replaces the parent's step in place; new names append."},{"kind":"section","title":"[image]","page":"Configuration Reference","path":"/docs/config#image","text":"Key Type Default Description · base string required Base container image (e.g. \"fedora:44\") · name string required Image tag name (e.g. \"myenv\") · image string — Prebuilt image reference (e.g. \"ghcr.io/user/myenv:latest\"). When set, podbox uses the registry image instead of building from base · pull_retry int 3 Number of pull retries on failure · pull_retry_delay string \"5s\" Delay between pull retries"},{"kind":"section","title":"[image.packages]","page":"Configuration Reference","path":"/docs/config#imagepackages","text":"Key Type Default Description · install string[] [] Packages to install via the distro package manager · remove string[] [] Packages to remove · manager string auto-detected Package manager override: dnf, apt, pacman, apk, zypper. Auto-detected from the base image name when omitted"},{"kind":"section","title":"[image.run]","page":"Configuration Reference","path":"/docs/config#imagerun","text":"Key Type Default Description · commands string[] [] Extra RUN commands in the Containerfile [image] base = \"fedora:44\" name = \"myenv\"  [image.packages] install = [\"git\", \"gcc\", \"ripgrep\"] remove = [\"vim-minimal\"] "},{"kind":"section","title":"[container]","page":"Configuration Reference","path":"/docs/config#container","text":"Key Type Default Description · name string required Container name (used for systemd unit names, socket paths) · home string required Host path for isolated home ( expands) · shell string \"bash\" Default login shell inside the container · memory string — Memory limit (e.g. \"4G\", \"2048M\"). Passed as Memory= in Quadlet · cpus string — CPU limit (e.g. \"2.0\", \"0.5\"). Passed through to Podman as --cpus · slice string \"podbox.slice\" systemd slice for the container service · cpu_weight integer 200 systemd CPU contention weight (1–10000) · reload_cmd string — Command run on config reload. Passed as ReloadCmd= in Quadlet"},{"kind":"section","title":"[container.mounts]","page":"Configuration Reference","path":"/docs/config#containermounts","text":"Key Type Default Description · extra string[] [] Extra Volume= lines (e.g. \"/Work:/home/user/Work:z\")"},{"kind":"section","title":"[container.env]","page":"Configuration Reference","path":"/docs/config#containerenv","text":"Key Type Default Description ·  string — Arbitrary environment variables passed to the container; explicit values always win over derived ones below · forward string[] [] Host variables to forward for enter, exec, and run; supports exact names and PREFIX patterns Derived variables (engine-set from the rest of the config, no TOML needed): Variable Derived from Example · SHELL container.shell (fish → /usr/bin/fish, bash → /bin/bash, zsh → /bin/zsh, full paths verbatim) shell = \"fish\" ⇒ SHELL=/usr/bin/fish · DISPLAY integration.x11 x11 = true ⇒ DISPLAY=:0 [container] name = \"myenv\" home = \"~/containers/myenv\" shell = \"zsh\"  [container.mounts] extra = [\"~/Projects:/home/user/Projects:z\"]  Services run under the guest daemon (the container's init stays Podman's). Short form restarts on failure; long form takes restart = \"never\", \"on-failure\", or \"always\", plus an env table. Logs go to /run/po"},{"kind":"section","title":"[security]","page":"Configuration Reference","path":"/docs/config#security","text":"Key Type Default Description · apparmor string — AppArmor profile name. Passed as AppArmor= in Quadlet (\"unconfined\" to disable) · seccomp string — Seccomp profile path, \"default\", or \"unconfined\". Passed as SeccompProfile= · security_label_disable bool true Disable SELinux process labeling. Emits SecurityLabelDisable=true when set · no_new_privileges bool true Block privilege escalation via setuid binaries (sudo, su, AUR helpers). Emits NoNewPrivileges=true in the Quadlet. Set false to allow. · read_only_rootfs bool false Make root filesystem read-only. Emits ReadOnly=true in Quadlet · userns string — User namespace mode override. Defaults to \"keep-id\". Supported: \"keep-id\", \"nomap\", \"private\" · cap_preset string \"default\" Capability preset. Options: \"none\", \"default\", \"monitoring\", \"admin\". Adds a predefined set of --cap-add entries alongside any cap_add list below · cap_add string[] ["},{"kind":"section","title":"[security].secrets","page":"Configuration Reference","path":"/docs/config#securitysecrets","text":"Values the container needs but the image must never contain. Short form reads a podman secret and exposes it as a same-named variable: [security] secrets = [\"openai_key\"]              # Secret=openai_key,type=env,target=openai_key Use the long form for a different target, a file instead of a variable, a mode, or a systemd credential source: [[security.secrets]] name = \"aws_creds\" type = \"mount\"                        # env (default) or mount target = \"/run/secrets/aws\"           # destination name inside the container mode = \"0400\"  [[security.secrets]] name = \"gh_token\" A systemd secret emits LoadCredential= : under [Service] (so %d/$CREDENTIALS_DIRECTORY is populated), mounts Volume=%d/ :/run/secrets/ :ro,z, and — for type = \"env\" — sets the target variable to the container path. mount secrets expose only the file. The credential name must be a safe filename (no / or ..). Key Type Defa"},{"kind":"section","title":"[network]","page":"Configuration Reference","path":"/docs/config#network","text":"Key Type Default Description · mode string \"pasta\" Network mode: \"host\", \"bridge\", \"none\", \"pasta\", \"slirp4netns\", \"private\". Defaults to \"pasta\" (user-space NAT with working networking). \"private\" is loopback only — no host sockets or localhost services. \"host\" shares the host network — choose it deliberately · ports string[] [] Port mappings (\"hostPort:containerPort\"). Emitted as PublishPort= in Quadlet (ignored in host mode) [network] mode = \"pasta\" ports = [\"8080:80\", \"443:443\"] offline = false offline = true forces Network=none while keeping your mode in the definition. Container-wide only — no per-command override."},{"kind":"section","title":"[storage.shared_caches]","page":"Configuration Reference","path":"/docs/config#storageshared_caches","text":"Opt-in caches shared between podbox containers. Removing a container keeps its volumes. [storage.shared_caches] cargo = true mbx = true rustup = false  [[storage.shared_caches.custom]] name = \"models\" container_path = \"~/.cache/models\" Key Type Default Path shared · cargo bool false /.cargo/registry, /.cargo/git · npm bool false /.npm · pnpm bool false /.local/share/pnpm/store · pip bool false /.cache/pip · uv bool false /.cache/uv · yarn bool false /.cache/yarn, /.yarn/berry/cache · bun bool false /.bun/install/cache · composer bool false /.cache/composer · maven bool false /.m2/repository · gradle bool false /.gradle/caches · ccache bool false /.cache/ccache · go bool false /go/pkg/mod · rustup bool false /.rustup · mbx bool false /.cache/mbx · custom[].name string — Built-in names are reserved · custom[].container_path string — Destination in the container, /… or absolute cargo shares"},{"kind":"section","title":"[storage.host_caches]","page":"Configuration Reference","path":"/docs/config#storagehost_caches","text":"Same toggles, different source: a directory you already keep on the host, bind-mounted into the container. Use this when the host owns the cache; use shared_caches when only containers use it. Same built-ins as shared_caches — same relative path on both sides. A cache whose host directory doesn't exist is skipped with a warning, not mounted: there is nothing on the host to reuse, and podman refuses to start a container whose bind source is missing. Create the directory (or run the tool once on the host) and re-enable to pick it up. [storage.host_caches] npm = true mbx = true  [[storage.host_caches.custom]] name = \"zig\" host_path = \"~/.cache/zig\" container_path = \"~/.cache/zig\" Key Type Default Description · cargo bool false /.cargo/registry and /.cargo/git · npm, pnpm, pip, uv, yarn, bun, composer, maven, gradle, ccache, go, rustup, mbx bool false That tool's cache; see the table above f"},{"kind":"section","title":"[integration]","page":"Configuration Reference","path":"/docs/config#integration","text":"Controls which host resources are shared with the container. Prebuilt images may ship OCI labels (podbox.integration.audio=true, …) suggesting integration defaults. Precedence is: an explicitly set definition value (including values from extends profiles) beats an image label, which beats the built-in default. Setting integration.audio = false therefore stays off even when the image label says true. Key Type Default Description · wayland bool false Share Wayland socket for GUI apps · x11 bool false Run X11 apps through a per-container xwayland-satellite bridge; requires wayland = true · audio bool false Share PipeWire/PulseAudio sockets · gpu string/bool \"auto\" GPU passthrough (true, false, \"auto\", \"nvidia\") · dbus bool false Enable D-Bus session bus access. Talk/own/preset rules without it are rejected; a rules set without the switch stated (a pre-0.9.0 file, where the bus defaulted on)"},{"kind":"section","title":"X11 applications","page":"Configuration Reference","path":"/docs/config#x11-applications","text":"With x11 = true, podbox runs one host-side xwayland-satellite service for the container and mounts only that service's X socket directory. Install both xwayland-satellite and Xwayland on the host; podbox does not add either to the image. The service uses the same Wayland socket as native container GUI applications, so x11 requires wayland = true and is compatible with network.offline = true. network.mode = \"host\" is rejected with X11 enabled. Host networking would expose the host network namespace's abstract X socket, allowing DISPLAY=:0 to connect to the host X server instead of this container's isolated server. All X11 clients inside one container share that container's X server and can observe each other's windows and input. They cannot access X servers belonging to other containers or the host through this bridge. X selections are bridged to the Wayland clipboard while an X11 window "},{"kind":"section","title":"GpuMode values","page":"Configuration Reference","path":"/docs/config#gpumode-values","text":"TOML value Meaning · \"auto\" (default) Detect available GPU devices at runtime · true Enable /dev/dri (Intel/AMD) · false Disable all GPU passthrough · \"nvidia\" Enable /dev/dri + NVIDIA passthrough: nvidia.com/gpu=all when a host CDI spec exists, otherwise all /dev/nvidia nodes (optional each; doctor suggests nvidia-ctk cdi generate when no CDI spec exists)"},{"kind":"section","title":"[integration.hardware]","page":"Configuration Reference","path":"/docs/config#integrationhardware","text":"Device passthrough. The container has no devices until you pass them here. Each becomes an optional AddDevice=-… — a host without it skips the device instead of failing to start. Key Type Default Passed through · kvm bool false /dev/kvm — nested virtualisation, Android emulators · joystick bool false /dev/input, /dev/uinput — gamepads and joysticks · webcam bool false /dev/video, /dev/media · serial bool false /dev/ttyUSB, /dev/ttyACM — microcontrollers · yubikey bool false pcscd socket and /dev/hidraw — smartcards, 2FA · usb bool false /dev/bus/usb (bind-mounted, hotplug-aware) — adb, fastboot, flashers, SDR [integration.hardware] webcam = true kvm = true podbox doctor reports when the host is missing a device these would need. USB access notes: the box sees exactly the devices your host user may open, so install the device's udev rules (android-tools / android-udev for phones) or join "},{"kind":"section","title":"[integration.host_exec]","page":"Configuration Reference","path":"/docs/config#integrationhost_exec","text":"Key Type Default Description · enabled bool false Allow container to execute commands on the host · allowlist table (none) Allowed commands as alias → path pairs. When set, only those commands may run; when absent, anything may run. Each value may also be a detailed table { path = \"/usr/bin/…\", filter = true, shim = true, timeout = 30 } · cwd string \"home\" What the fd-passing session does with the guest working directory (see cwd handling below) cwd handling The fd-passing session carries the shell's working directory in HostExecV2.cwd; cwd decides what the host does with it before the command runs. The policy applies to tty sessions the same way — an interactive session can be directed to its host project directory, or refused, exactly like a piped one: Value Behaviour · \"home\" (default) Ignore the guest path: the command starts in the host $HOME · \"map\" Translate the guest path through"},{"kind":"section","title":"[integration.xdg_dirs]","page":"Configuration Reference","path":"/docs/config#integrationxdg_dirs","text":"Key Type Default Description · documents bool false Mount host /Documents · downloads bool false Mount host /Downloads · pictures bool false Mount host /Pictures · music bool false Mount host /Music · videos bool false Mount host /Videos · desktop bool false Mount host /Desktop · projects bool false Mount host /Projects A directory can be mounted read-write with the detailed form, which is written compactly under its parent: projects = { enabled = true, read_write = true }."},{"kind":"section","title":"[integration.export]","page":"Configuration Reference","path":"/docs/config#integrationexport","text":"Key Type Default Description · apps string[] [] App .desktop files to export (without .desktop suffix) · bins string[] [] Binary shims to generate in /.local/bin [integration] wayland    = true audio      = true gpu        = \"auto\" dbus       = true notify     = true xdg_open   = true clipboard  = true"},{"kind":"section","title":"[lifecycle]","page":"Configuration Reference","path":"/docs/config#lifecycle","text":"Key Type Default Description · quadlet bool false Generate Quadlet systemd files on podbox enable · autostart bool false Start container on user login (WantedBy=default.target) · on_stop string \"keep\" Deprecated, no effect: Quadlet containers are always ephemeral (podman run --rm --replace); state lives in the home volume and snapshots. Setting it prints a deprecation warning · auto_update bool false Add Label=io.containers.autoupdate=registry for auto-updates · auto_checkpoint bool false Tag the current image as checkpoint-prev before update or build --rebuild; podbox rollback restores that image · idle_timeout string \"off\" Idle timeout before guest daemon exits (\"off\", \"30s\", \"5m\", \"1h\") [lifecycle] quadlet      = true autostart    = true auto_update  = true idle_timeout = \"off\""},{"kind":"section","title":"[systemd]","page":"Configuration Reference","path":"/docs/config#systemd","text":"Custom systemd unit dependencies for the generated Quadlet. Key Type Default Description · requires string[] [] Units that must be active before the container (Requires=) · after string[] [] Units the container should start after (After=) [systemd] requires = [\"postgres.service\", \"redis.service\"] after    = [\"network-online.target\"]"},{"kind":"section","title":"[dbus]","page":"Configuration Reference","path":"/docs/config#dbus","text":"D-Bus access control via xdg-dbus-proxy. Requires integration.dbus = true. Key Type Default Description · preset string \"none\" Preset filling talk for you: \"flatpak\", \"gnome\", \"kde\", \"portal\" (adds org.freedesktop.Notifications talk). Any preset other than \"none\" implies the proxy — unknown values are a parse error. Portal names are never granted via talk — they come through interface-scoped rules for notify / xdg_open (see dbus-proxy.md) · talk string[] [] D-Bus services the container can call (two-way). Adding a portal-family name re-grants the full portal surface — a warning is printed · own string[] [] D-Bus services the container can register on the host bus [dbus] preset = \"gnome\" [dbus] preset = \"portal\" talk = [     \"org.freedesktop.Notifications\",     \"org.mpris.MediaPlayer2.*\", ] own = [     \"org.mpris.MediaPlayer2.podbox_app\", See dbus-proxy.md for the full behavior matrix."},{"kind":"section","title":"[wayland]","page":"Configuration Reference","path":"/docs/config#wayland","text":"The Wayland firewall. A companion service filters which protocol objects the container may use — screen capture, virtual input, and input methods are blocked unless you allow them. Key Type Default Description · firewall bool true Filter Wayland protocol access through the compositor proxy · blocked_interfaces string[] (see below) Wayland globals to deny. Replaces the default list when set [wayland] firewall = true blocked_interfaces = [     \"zwlr_screencopy_manager_v1\",     \"ext_image_copy_capture_v1\", ] Setting blocked_interfaces replaces the default list entirely — no merging. The default list is also what a definition without [wayland] gets, so the firewall blocks the same global set whether or not the table appears in the file; it is written out only when you replace it. Screen capture (zwlr_screencopy_manager_v1, ext_image_copy_capture_v1), window listing (ext_foreign_toplevel_list"},{"kind":"section","title":"Full Example","page":"Configuration Reference","path":"/docs/config#full-example","text":"This is a reference example showing every available key with sane defaults. It is not a working config — most install lists, env vars, and mounts are placeholders. Pick only what you need; omitted keys use their defaults. Files podbox writes (create, init, the Studio) go further: a section that holds only default values is not written at all, so a minimal definition stays minimal — e.g. enabling nothing under [integration] produces no [integration] header, and the wayland firewall prints its block list only when you change it. # ── Image ────────────────────────────────────────────── [image] base = \"fedora:44\"              # Base image for custom builds name = \"myenv\"                  # Image tag name image = \"ghcr.io/user/myenv:latest\"  # Prebuilt ref (omit for custom builds) pull_retry = 3                  # Pull retry count pull_retry_delay = \"5s\"         # Delay between pull retries "},{"kind":"key","title":"Configuration.talk","page":"D-Bus Proxy","path":"/docs/dbus-proxy#configuration","text":"string[] · D-Bus services the container can call (two-way communication)"},{"kind":"key","title":"Configuration.own","page":"D-Bus Proxy","path":"/docs/dbus-proxy#configuration","text":"string[] · D-Bus services the container can register on the host bus"},{"kind":"page","title":"D-Bus Proxy","page":"D-Bus Proxy","path":"/docs/dbus-proxy","text":"Filtered D-Bus access for podbox containers via xdg-dbus-proxy — configuration, allowed services, and security model."},{"kind":"section","title":"How it works","page":"D-Bus Proxy","path":"/docs/dbus-proxy#how-it-works","text":"When [dbus] talk/own rules are configured, or notify/xdg_open need portal access: podbox enable writes an additional file: /.config/containers/systemd/ -proxy.service · The generated .container quadlet gains: Requires= -proxy.service After= -proxy.service · Instead of Volume=%t/bus:%t/bus, the container gets the proxy socket: Volume=%t/podbox/ -dbus.sock:/run/podbox/dbus.sock:ro Environment=DBUS_SESSION_BUS_ADDRESS=unix:path=/run/podbox/dbus.sock · The proxy service runs xdg-dbus-proxy, which forwards only the explicitly allowed D-Bus services to the container."},{"kind":"section","title":"Configuration","page":"D-Bus Proxy","path":"/docs/dbus-proxy#configuration","text":"[dbus] talk = [     \"org.freedesktop.Notifications\",     \"org.mpris.MediaPlayer2.*\", ] own = [     \"org.mpris.MediaPlayer2.podbox_app\", ] Key Type Description · talk string[] D-Bus services the container can call (two-way communication) · own string[] D-Bus services the container can register on the host bus Wildcards () are supported per the xdg-dbus-proxy filtering rules. Warning: adding org.freedesktop.portal. (or any org.freedesktop.portal. / org.freedesktop.impl.portal. name) to talk re-grants the full portal bus surface, including host-privileged interfaces like DynamicLauncher, Screenshot, ScreenCast and Settings. Prefer the built-in interface-scoped rules described below; podbox prints a warning when it sees a portal-family talk entry."},{"kind":"section","title":"Portal access model","page":"D-Bus Proxy","path":"/docs/dbus-proxy#portal-access-model","text":"The portal preset (applied by default when [dbus] has no explicit rules) does not add org.freedesktop.portal. to the talk list. Instead, the generated proxy exposes org.freedesktop.portal.Desktop through interface-scoped rules, one per enabled capability: Capability Rule granted · integration.notify --call=org.freedesktop.portal.Desktop=org.freedesktop.portal.Notification.@/org/freedesktop/portal/desktop · integration.xdg_open --call=org.freedesktop.portal.Desktop=org.freedesktop.portal.OpenURI.@/org/freedesktop/portal/desktop · either --call=org.freedesktop.portal.Desktop=org.freedesktop.portal.Request.@/org/freedesktop/portal/desktop/request/ (async Request pattern, incl. Request.Close) · either --broadcast=org.freedesktop.portal.Desktop=org.freedesktop.portal.Request.@/org/freedesktop/portal/desktop/request/ (Request.Response result signals) · either --call=org.freedesktop.portal.Desk"},{"kind":"section","title":"Behavior matrix","page":"D-Bus Proxy","path":"/docs/dbus-proxy#behavior-matrix","text":"integration.dbus [dbus] config What the container gets · false any No D-Bus access · true default (empty) preset = \"portal\" is applied: proxied via xdg-dbus-proxy · true any preset set (flatpak, gnome, kde, portal) Proxied via xdg-dbus-proxy with the preset's talk names plus interface-scoped portal rules for the enabled capabilities · true preset = \"none\", empty talk + own, no capabilities on Unfiltered Volume=%t/bus:%t/bus (explicit opt-out of filtering)"},{"kind":"section","title":"Generated proxy unit","page":"D-Bus Proxy","path":"/docs/dbus-proxy#generated-proxy-unit","text":"When rules are present, a companion systemd service is generated at /.config/containers/systemd/ -proxy.service: [Unit] Description=D-Bus Proxy for podbox container <name> PartOf=<name>.service  [Service] Type=simple RuntimeDirectory=podbox ExecStart=/usr/bin/xdg-dbus-proxy \\ Stopping the container stops the proxy; restarting restarts it (PartOf= .service)."},{"kind":"section","title":"Requirements","page":"D-Bus Proxy","path":"/docs/dbus-proxy#requirements","text":"xdg-dbus-proxy must be installed on the host system (package xdg-dbus-proxy, commonly shipped with Flatpak) · integration.dbus = true (the master switch) · A D-Bus session bus socket must be present on the host (auto-detected)"},{"kind":"section","title":"Verification","page":"D-Bus Proxy","path":"/docs/dbus-proxy#verification","text":""},{"kind":"section","title":"Test an allowed service","page":"D-Bus Proxy","path":"/docs/dbus-proxy#test-an-allowed-service","text":"gdbus call --session \\     --dest org.freedesktop.Notifications \\     --object-path /org/freedesktop/Notifications \\     --method org.freedesktop.Notifications.Notify \\     \"podbox\" 0 \"\" \"Hello\" \"Proxied message.\" [] {} 5000 Should succeed and show a host notification."},{"kind":"section","title":"Test isolation","page":"D-Bus Proxy","path":"/docs/dbus-proxy#test-isolation","text":"gdbus call --session \\     --dest org.freedesktop.systemd1 \\     --object-path /org/freedesktop/systemd1 \\     --method org.freedesktop.DBus.Peer.Ping Should fail with access denied — the proxy blocks unapproved services."},{"kind":"section","title":"Portal surface audit","page":"D-Bus Proxy","path":"/docs/dbus-proxy#portal-surface-audit","text":"Audited from inside a container against the proxied socket. Allowed: Interface Result · org.freedesktop.portal.Notification.AddNotification () (host notification shown) · org.freedesktop.portal.OpenURI.OpenURI request handle returned · org.freedesktop.DBus.Introspectable.Introspect introspection XML (needed by GIO clients) Interface · Screenshot.Screenshot · ScreenCast.CreateSession · RemoteDesktop.CreateSession · InputCapture.CreateSession · Settings.Read · Documents.Add · Account.GetUserInformation · GameMode.QueryStatus · Lockdown.GetDisabled · Print.PreparePrint · Wallpaper.SetWallpaperURI · DynamicLauncher.RequestInstallToken · FileChooser.OpenFile Denial happens at the proxy before anything reaches the host — screen capture, screenshots, file access, and launcher install stay unreachable unless the matching capability is enabled."},{"kind":"page","title":"Desktop Integration (Export)","page":"Desktop Integration (Export)","path":"/docs/export","text":"Export apps and binaries from podbox containers to your host desktop — .desktop files, launcher icons, binary shims, and MIME type handling."},{"kind":"section","title":"Commands","page":"Desktop Integration (Export)","path":"/docs/export#commands","text":"Command Description · podbox export app Export a .desktop application · podbox export bin Create a binary shim in /.local/bin Applications and binaries are declared in the config under [integration.export]: [integration.export] apps = [\"gedit\", \"nautilus\"] bins = [\"rg\", \"gcc\"]"},{"kind":"section","title":"App Export","page":"Desktop Integration (Export)","path":"/docs/export#app-export","text":"podbox export app extracts a desktop application from the container and makes it launchable from the host. !!! info \"\" The container must be running to export an app — podbox export app uses podman exec to read the .desktop file from inside the container. Start the container first if it is stopped."},{"kind":"section","title":"How it works","page":"Desktop Integration (Export)","path":"/docs/export#how-it-works","text":"Reads .desktop from the running container: the system application dirs first, then the container user's /.local/share/applications, then /opt//share/applications. · Rewrites Exec= to route through podbox (everything else preserved). TryExec= is dropped (it points inside the container and would hide the launcher) and Icon= is pointed at the copied host icon below: ini Exec=gedit %F becomes: Exec=podbox --container \"myenv\" exec -- gedit %F · Copies the first matching icon to /.local/share/icons/podbox/ / . . · Writes /.local/share/applications/podbox- - .desktop. · Runs update-desktop-database (a failure only warns). /usr/share/icons/hicolor/{48,64,128,256}x{48,64,128,256}/apps/<name>.png /usr/share/icons/hicolor/scalable/apps/<name>.svg"},{"kind":"section","title":"MIME types","page":"Desktop Integration (Export)","path":"/docs/export#mime-types","text":"MimeType= lines carry over unchanged — your desktop registers the container app as a handler, and opening such a file dispatches through the rewritten Exec=. No extra setup."},{"kind":"section","title":"Binary Export","page":"Desktop Integration (Export)","path":"/docs/export#binary-export","text":"podbox export bin creates a shell shim so a container binary appears on the host PATH."},{"kind":"section","title":"Generated shim","page":"Desktop Integration (Export)","path":"/docs/export#generated-shim","text":"A script is written to /.local/bin/ : #!/bin/sh exec podbox --container \"<name>\" run \"<bin>\" \"$@\" The --container flag pins the target regardless of active context. If /.local/bin is on your PATH (most distros do this), the binary just works."},{"kind":"section","title":"Cleanup","page":"Desktop Integration (Export)","path":"/docs/export#cleanup","text":"podbox export clean Removes the container's .desktop files, its icon dir, and any /.local/bin shims referencing it. !!! warning \"\" podbox remove doesn't unexport. Clean up before removing the container."},{"kind":"page","title":"Getting Started","page":"Getting Started","path":"/docs/getting-started","text":"Get started with podbox — prebuilt profiles, custom builds, and daily usage."},{"kind":"section","title":"Installation","page":"Getting Started","path":"/docs/getting-started#installation","text":"curl -fsSL https://bethropolis.github.io/podbox/install.sh | sh mise # Install as a mise tool (Linux only) mise use -g github:bethropolis/podbox Homebrew # Homebrew (Linux only) brew install bethropolis/homebrew-tap/podbox Arch Linux, via AUR # Arch Linux, via AUR (binary, fast) paru -S podbox-bin  # ...or build from source paru -S podbox From crates.io (supports prebuilt images only) # From crates.io (supports prebuilt images only) cargo install podbox-cli Source install (builds CLI and guest daemon) # Source install (builds CLI & guest daemon) git clone https://github.com/bethropolis/podbox cd podbox && scripts/install.sh   # installs to ~/.local/bin The crates.io build supports prebuilt images only. Custom image builds need a full source build."},{"kind":"section","title":"Before you start","page":"Getting Started","path":"/docs/getting-started#before-you-start","text":"podbox needs three things: Podman 5.5 or newer. Use 5.6+ if you want SSH agent passthrough. · A systemd user session. podbox hands the container lifecycle to systemd. · Linux with a Wayland compositor. X11 apps still work through Xwayland. Optionally, install xdg-dbus-proxy to get filtered D-Bus access instead of an open session bus. For X11 applications, also install the host dependencies xwayland-satellite and Xwayland. Enable them per container with integration.x11 = true. If any of that is missing, podbox doctor will tell you."},{"kind":"section","title":"Two Ways to Create a Container","page":"Getting Started","path":"/docs/getting-started#two-ways-to-create-a-container","text":"podbox supports two workflows depending on how much control you need: Method Use case How it works · Prebuilt Trying things out, gaming Pull a ready-made image. Everything is baked in — just create and enter. · Custom Your own packages and setup Start from a distro image and declare the rest in TOML. podbox generates the build."},{"kind":"section","title":"Prebuilt (Quick Start)","page":"Getting Started","path":"/docs/getting-started#prebuilt-quick-start","text":"Prebuilt profiles come with Wayland, audio, GPU, and common packages ready to go. They're the fastest way to get a working container."},{"kind":"section","title":"Available profiles","page":"Getting Started","path":"/docs/getting-started#available-profiles","text":"Profile Base Use case · cachy CachyOS (Arch-based) Gaming, general purpose · fedora Fedora Development, general purpose · dev Fedora Development tooling, focused toolset Run podbox profile list to see the full list."},{"kind":"section","title":"Non-interactive","page":"Getting Started","path":"/docs/getting-started#non-interactive","text":"# Create a gaming-ready container podbox create cachy  # Or a Fedora-based one with a custom name podbox create fedora --name dev"},{"kind":"section","title":"Interactive","page":"Getting Started","path":"/docs/getting-started#interactive","text":"# Launch the wizard and pick a profile podbox init -i  # After the wizard finishes, create the container podbox create dev"},{"kind":"section","title":"Verify it works","page":"Getting Started","path":"/docs/getting-started#verify-it-works","text":"# List all podbox containers podbox list  # Check status podbox status cachy  # Run diagnostics podbox doctor"},{"kind":"section","title":"What happens","page":"Getting Started","path":"/docs/getting-started#what-happens","text":"podbox init creates a config file at /.config/podbox/profiles/ .toml · podbox create pulls the prebuilt image, writes Quadlet systemd files, and starts the container · The guest daemon (podbox-guest) starts inside and connects to the host for notifications, clipboard, and URI forwarding · The container is running and ready — podbox enter drops you into a shell"},{"kind":"section","title":"Custom (Build from Base)","page":"Getting Started","path":"/docs/getting-started#custom-build-from-base","text":"Build a container from a plain distro image with your own packages, shell, and configuration."},{"kind":"section","title":"Non-interactive","page":"Getting Started","path":"/docs/getting-started#non-interactive-1","text":"# Create a config from a base image podbox init fedora:44 --name myenv  # Build the image, enable Quadlet, and start podbox create myenv  # Jump in podbox enter myenv Or in one step with create: # podbox create works with any OCI image reference podbox create myenv podbox create ubuntu:26.04 --name dev"},{"kind":"section","title":"Interactive","page":"Getting Started","path":"/docs/getting-started#interactive-1","text":"# Launch the interactive wizard podbox init -i  # Select \"Custom (from scratch)\" at the top of the list # Choose: base image, packages to install, extra RUN commands # Complete the wizard (shell, XDG dirs, GPU, lifecycle)  # Build and start"},{"kind":"section","title":"Container naming","page":"Getting Started","path":"/docs/getting-started#container-naming","text":"When podbox init is called without --name, the container name is derived from the image tag: Image ref Container name · fedora:44 fedora-44 · fedora:latest fedora · ubuntu:26.04 ubuntu-26-04 · ghcr.io/user/img:v1 img-v1 This avoids name conflicts when creating containers from different tags of the same base image. Use --name to override explicitly."},{"kind":"section","title":"Custom config example","page":"Getting Started","path":"/docs/getting-started#custom-config-example","text":"# ~/.config/podbox/myenv.toml [image] base = \"fedora:44\" name = \"myenv\"  [image.packages] install = [\"git\", \"neovim\", \"gcc\", \"ripgrep\"]  !!! tip \"\" Empty or default sections ([lifecycle], [dbus], [container.env], etc.) are omitted automatically — the generated TOML stays concise."},{"kind":"section","title":"Inspect what was generated","page":"Getting Started","path":"/docs/getting-started#inspect-what-was-generated","text":"# View the resolved TOML config podbox inspect myenv --toml  # View the generated Quadlet systemd units podbox inspect myenv --quadlet  # View the computed environment podbox inspect myenv --env"},{"kind":"section","title":"Check for drift","page":"Getting Started","path":"/docs/getting-started#check-for-drift","text":"After installing packages manually inside the container, see what differs from your config: podbox diff myenv Use --apply to update the config TOML's install list to match the running container: podbox diff myenv --apply"},{"kind":"section","title":"What happens","page":"Getting Started","path":"/docs/getting-started#what-happens-1","text":"podbox init creates a config file at /.config/podbox/profiles/ .toml · podbox build auto-generates a Containerfile from the config, copies in the guest binary (podbox-guest), and runs podman build · podbox enable writes Quadlet files ( .container, .socket, -host.service) to /.config/containers/systemd/ · podbox start starts the container — the guest daemon connects to the host socket · podbox enter opens an interactive shell"},{"kind":"section","title":"Daily Usage","page":"Getting Started","path":"/docs/getting-started#daily-usage","text":""},{"kind":"section","title":"Active context","page":"Getting Started","path":"/docs/getting-started#active-context","text":"Set a default container so bare commands \"just work\": # Set myenv as the active context podbox use myenv  # All commands now target myenv podbox status podbox logs podbox exec -- htop To target a different container, pass the name explicitly: podbox status fedora podbox enter fedora"},{"kind":"section","title":"Open a shell","page":"Getting Started","path":"/docs/getting-started#open-a-shell","text":"podbox enter myenv podbox shell myenv Both work. shell is an alias for enter."},{"kind":"section","title":"Run commands","page":"Getting Started","path":"/docs/getting-started#run-commands","text":"# Run interactively inside the container podbox exec -- htop podbox exec -- cargo build  # Run as root podbox exec --root -- apt update  # Launch a GUI app (detached)"},{"kind":"section","title":"Export to host","page":"Getting Started","path":"/docs/getting-started#export-to-host","text":"Make container apps and binaries available on the host: # Add Firefox to your host launcher podbox export app firefox  # Make ripgrep available as a host command podbox export bin rg  # Remove all exports for the current container podbox export clean !!! tip \"\" Exported .desktop files go to /.local/share/applications/ and binary shims to /.local/bin/."},{"kind":"section","title":"Resource usage","page":"Getting Started","path":"/docs/getting-started#resource-usage","text":"# Show real-time resource usage podbox stats  # Single snapshot, no streaming podbox stats --no-stream  # JSON output for scripting podbox stats --output json"},{"kind":"section","title":"Snapshots","page":"Getting Started","path":"/docs/getting-started#snapshots","text":"Commit the current container state and roll back if needed: # Tag the current state (defaults to timestamp tag) podbox snapshot create myenv  # Tag with a custom name podbox snapshot create myenv --tag before-upgrade  # List snapshots podbox snapshot list myenv"},{"kind":"section","title":"Path translation","page":"Getting Started","path":"/docs/getting-started#path-translation","text":"Find the equivalent path between host and container: # Host → container podbox translate-path --to-container ~/Projects/myapp  # Container → host podbox translate-path --to-host /home/user/Projects/myapp"},{"kind":"section","title":"Troubleshoot","page":"Getting Started","path":"/docs/getting-started#troubleshoot","text":"# Run diagnostics podbox doctor  # Auto-fix common issues (Wayland socket ownership, etc.) podbox doctor --fix"},{"kind":"section","title":"Lifecycle Management","page":"Getting Started","path":"/docs/getting-started#lifecycle-management","text":""},{"kind":"section","title":"Understanding the chain","page":"Getting Started","path":"/docs/getting-started#understanding-the-chain","text":"podbox uses four stages: podbox build    # Build the container image from the TOML config podbox enable   # Write Quadlet systemd files (~/.config/containers/systemd/) podbox start    # Start the container podbox enter    # Open a shell podbox create runs all of these in one command."},{"kind":"section","title":"Preview without side effects","page":"Getting Started","path":"/docs/getting-started#preview-without-side-effects","text":"# See what would happen without executing anything podbox build --dry-run podbox enable --dry-run"},{"kind":"section","title":"Quadlet persistence","page":"Getting Started","path":"/docs/getting-started#quadlet-persistence","text":"When Quadlet is enabled ([lifecycle] quadlet = true), systemd manages the container: It starts automatically on login (WantedBy=default.target) · It restarts on crash (Restart=on-failure) · The socket is created before the container and persists across restarts"},{"kind":"section","title":"Start and stop","page":"Getting Started","path":"/docs/getting-started#start-and-stop","text":"# Start podbox start myenv  # Stop (container stays, can be started again) podbox stop myenv  # Disable and remove Quadlet files podbox disable myenv"},{"kind":"section","title":"Remove","page":"Getting Started","path":"/docs/getting-started#remove","text":"# Remove the container only (config stays) podbox remove myenv  # Remove container and home directory podbox remove myenv --all  # Force-remove without confirmation podbox remove myenv --all --force"},{"kind":"section","title":"Update and rebuild","page":"Getting Started","path":"/docs/getting-started#update-and-rebuild","text":"# Rebuild the image (picks up config changes automatically) podbox build  # Force a full rebuild from scratch podbox build --rebuild  # Pull latest image and restart (prebuilt containers) podbox update myenv"},{"kind":"section","title":"Edit config interactively","page":"Getting Started","path":"/docs/getting-started#edit-config-interactively","text":"# Open the config in your editor podbox edit myenv  # After saving, rebuild if the image config changed podbox edit myenv --rebuild"},{"kind":"section","title":"Commands at a Glance","page":"Getting Started","path":"/docs/getting-started#commands-at-a-glance","text":""},{"kind":"section","title":"Profiles","page":"Getting Started","path":"/docs/getting-started#profiles","text":"Command Description · podbox profile list List all available profiles (bundled + custom) · podbox profile show Show the TOML configuration of a profile · podbox profile export Print a profile's TOML (--output to write it) · podbox profile import Install a TOML file into profiles/ (--name, --force) Your own profiles/ .toml always wins over a bundled profile of the same name, so podbox create dev builds your file — never the template. To inherit from the template you shadowed, extend builtin: (e.g. extends = \"builtin:dev\"): profile: would resolve back to your own file and fail as circular."},{"kind":"section","title":"Creating and building","page":"Getting Started","path":"/docs/getting-started#creating-and-building","text":"Command Description · podbox init Scaffold a config from the default base image (fedora:44) · podbox profile list List available profiles (bundled + custom) · podbox init Scaffold a custom config from a base image · podbox init -i Interactive wizard (custom or profile) · podbox init --profile Scaffold from a prebuilt profile · podbox create Init → build → enable → start in one step (on-disk config wins over bundled profile) · podbox create --name Pull + create config + enable + start · podbox create --import Install a TOML file into profiles/ and create from it · podbox create --edit Open the config in your editor first, then create from what you saved · podbox build [ ] Build or rebuild the container image · podbox pull Pull a prebuilt image without building"},{"kind":"section","title":"Running and entering","page":"Getting Started","path":"/docs/getting-started#running-and-entering","text":"Command Description · podbox enter [ ] Enter a running container (auto-starts) · podbox shell [ ] Open an interactive shell · podbox exec -- Execute a command · podbox run Launch a GUI app (detached)"},{"kind":"section","title":"Managing state","page":"Getting Started","path":"/docs/getting-started#managing-state","text":"Command Description · podbox enable [ ] Install Quadlet systemd files · podbox disable [ ] [--force] Remove Quadlet files · podbox start [ ] Start the container · podbox stop [ ] Stop the container · podbox remove [ ] [--all] Remove the container (and home with --all) · podbox remove --stale Clean up orphaned/failed containers · podbox snapshot create [ ] [--tag ] Commit container state as an OCI image · podbox snapshot list [ ] List snapshots for a container · podbox restore [ ] Roll back to a previous snapshot · podbox rollback [ ] Restore the image captured before the last update or rebuild · podbox clone Copy a config for a variant · podbox update [ ] Pull latest image and restart"},{"kind":"section","title":"Exporting","page":"Getting Started","path":"/docs/getting-started#exporting","text":"Command Description · podbox export app Export a .desktop file to the host launcher · podbox export bin Create a binary shim in /.local/bin · podbox export clean Remove all exported shims and .desktop files"},{"kind":"section","title":"Diagnostics and utilities","page":"Getting Started","path":"/docs/getting-started#diagnostics-and-utilities","text":"Command Description · podbox status [ ] Show container state · podbox logs [ ] [-f] [--since ] Show container logs · podbox stats [ ] Show resource usage · podbox diff [ ] Compare installed packages against config · podbox doctor [--fix] Diagnose and fix common issues · podbox use [ ] [--clear] Set or show the active context · podbox find-definition [ ] Print path to the matching config TOML · podbox list List all podbox-managed containers · podbox inspect [ ] Show resolved config, Quadlet, or environment · podbox edit [ ] Open the config in your editor · podbox translate-path --to-container Translate a host path to container path · podbox translate-path --to-host Translate a container path to host path · podbox completions Generate shell completions · podbox dotfiles sync [ ] Update dotfiles and rerun their install command · podbox dotfiles status [ ] Show dotfiles provisioning state · "},{"kind":"section","title":"Next Steps","page":"Getting Started","path":"/docs/getting-started#next-steps","text":"Configuration Reference — all TOML keys, defaults, and examples · Home Setup — dotfiles and one-shot provisioning steps · Architecture Overview — how podbox works end-to-end · Desktop Integration — exporting apps and binaries · Troubleshooting Guide — common issues and fixes"},{"kind":"page","title":"Guest daemon","page":"Guest daemon","path":"/docs/guest","text":"podbox-guest daemon internals — startup sequence, event loop, socket protocol, and interceptors for notifications, clipboard, xdg-open, and host-exec."},{"kind":"section","title":"Entry point","page":"Guest daemon","path":"/docs/guest#entry-point","text":"The container starts with podbox-guest --entry [ ...]: Symlinks /run/user/%U/flatpak-info → /.flatpak-info, so portal-aware toolkits route audio/video capture through portals. · fork()s: the child re-execs podbox-guest --daemon; the parent execs your command (or a login shell). · The daemon idles in the background with a 5-minute timeout."},{"kind":"section","title":"User setup and privilege drop","page":"Guest daemon","path":"/docs/guest#user-setup-and-privilege-drop","text":"When running as root with HOST_USER/HOST_UID/HOST_GID set, the entrypoint creates (or reconciles) the container user before exec: An existing name with a different UID/GID is moved onto the host's ids with usermod; a UID taken by another name is freed with userdel -r (logged). Either mismatch without reconciliation would leave setuid on an id with no passwd entry (\"I have no name!\"). · The user joins wheel/sudo, video, audio, render and input wherever those groups exist, so GPU and device access granted by group keeps working. · Sudo goes to /etc/sudoers.d/podbox- (mode 0440), and the password is deleted (passwd -d) so su works on sudo-less images. · The drop itself is fail-closed: initgroups → setgid → setuid, verified against getuid/geteuid/getgid. Any failure aborts the entrypoint instead of running the shell as root. · The login shell comes from passwd after reconciliation, falling b"},{"kind":"section","title":"Daemon startup","page":"Guest daemon","path":"/docs/guest#daemon-startup","text":"Creates /run/podbox/bin/ for interceptor symlinks. · Compares PODBOX_HOST_VERSION against its own version; warns on drift. · Connects to the host socket (3 tries, 500ms apart). · Handshakes — sends its capabilities, gets back the accepted subset. · Symlinks one interceptor per accepted capability. · Prepends /run/podbox/bin to PATH via /etc/profile.d/podbox.sh and /etc/fish/conf.d/podbox.fish. · Enters the event loop: poll() on the socket, 0% CPU when idle. With lifecycle.idle_timeout set, it exits once no user processes remain past the timeout."},{"kind":"section","title":"Event loop","page":"Guest daemon","path":"/docs/guest#event-loop","text":"Event Action · Shutdown Exit · Ping No-op (keepalive) · CheckIdle Scan /proc; reply Busy or IdleTimeout · Disconnect / POLLHUP / POLLERR Exit · Idle timeout expired Send IdleTimeout, exit · EINTR Retry poll() User processes are tracked via pidfds (Linux 5.3+). 0% CPU when idle."},{"kind":"section","title":"Socket protocol","page":"Guest daemon","path":"/docs/guest#socket-protocol","text":"Length-prefixed JSON over $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/podbox/ .sock. Full wire format: protocol.md. → {\"type\":\"hello\",\"version\":\"0.1.0\",\"container\":\"myenv\",\"capabilities\":[\"notify\",\"xdg_open\",\"clipboard\",\"host_exec\"]} ← {\"type\":\"hello_ack\",\"accepted\":[\"notify\",\"xdg_open\"],\"rejected\":[\"clipboard\",\"host_exec\"]}"},{"kind":"section","title":"Interceptors","page":"Guest daemon","path":"/docs/guest#interceptors","text":""},{"kind":"section","title":"Symlink dispatch","page":"Guest daemon","path":"/docs/guest#symlink-dispatch","text":"Symlinks in /run/podbox/bin/, one per accepted capability. The binary reads argv[0] to know which interceptor it is. They shadow system binaries via PATH (/etc/profile.d/podbox.sh, /etc/fish/conf.d/podbox.fish). Symlink Capability What it does · notify-send notify Forwards args; --action/-A buttons wait for the host's reply · xdg-open xdg_open Sends the URI to the host · podbox-clipboard clipboard set reads stdin; get writes the host clipboard to stdout · host-exec host_exec Runs the command on the host, relays output as it arrives, exits with its code Each opens its own short-lived socket connection, sends one message, waits for the reply, exits. xdg-open is the one that waits for the host to close the connection: a clean close exits 0, and a refusal exits 1 with the reason."},{"kind":"section","title":"xdg-open scheme allowlist","page":"Guest daemon","path":"/docs/guest#xdg-open-scheme-allowlist","text":"The host opens only http, https and mailto URIs by default. Anything else is refused with an error the guest prints, exit 1. Refused include file:, smb:, sftp:, trash:, archive: and app handlers such as vscode: or steam:. A bare hostname such as example.com is opened as https://. Control characters, URIs over 8 KiB and http(s) URLs without a host are refused too. The allowlist is a footgun by design: a container's xdg-open is the host declining to run unknown handlers on URLs a container found. If you widen integration.xdg_open.allowed_schemes, a file: or vscode: URL from inside the container now reaches the host's handler for that scheme — opening local files or launching editor windows. Only add schemes you understand the host-side side effects of (for example smb: paired with an allowlisted talk daemon like a VFS client); the web schemes and mailto stay refused when they would otherwi"},{"kind":"section","title":"Host-exec security","page":"Guest daemon","path":"/docs/guest#host-exec-security","text":"Off by default. When enabled, the host validates every command: Check Rejected Example error · Allowlist Anything not in the map (guest $PATH ignored) Permission denied: 'ls' is not in the host-exec allowlist · Shell metacharacters ;, |, &, $, in args host-exec: failed to execute 'echo $HOME' · Dangerous flags --exec-path, --config, -o, … Security violation: argument \"--exec-path=/tmp/x\" … · Absolute-path bypass /usr/bin/git when the key is git Permission denied: '/usr/bin/git' is not in the host-exec allowlist See config.md for the allowlist shape — and prefer wrapper scripts over general-purpose tools."},{"kind":"page","title":"Documentation","page":"Documentation","path":"/docs","text":""},{"kind":"section","title":"User Guides","page":"Documentation","path":"/docs#user-guides","text":"Doc What it covers · Quick Start Install, create a container, essential workflows · Configuration Reference All TOML keys, defaults, and examples · Base packages Tools, shells, locales preinstalled in custom builds · Desktop Integration Exporting container apps and binaries to the host · Container Integration How the guest daemon bridges notifications, URI opening, clipboard · D-Bus Proxy Filtered D-Bus access via xdg-dbus-proxy · Home Setup Dotfiles and provisioning steps inside the container home"},{"kind":"section","title":"Reference","page":"Documentation","path":"/docs#reference","text":"Doc What it covers · Architecture Overview How podbox works end-to-end · Quadlet Keys Generated systemd unit files · Host-Guest Protocol Wire format and message types · Exit Codes Program exit code meanings"},{"kind":"section","title":"Quick Reference","page":"Documentation","path":"/docs#quick-reference","text":"podbox use <name>                # set active context podbox profile list              # list available profiles podbox profile show <name>       # show profile TOML podbox create <profile>          # create + build + enable + start podbox enter <name>              # open a shell podbox exec -- <cmd>             # run a command podbox run <app>                 # run a GUI app podbox stats                     # show resource usage Most commands accept an optional — defaults to the active context. See cli.md for name resolution, exit codes, and JSON output."},{"kind":"page","title":"Host-Guest Socket Protocol","page":"Host-Guest Socket Protocol","path":"/docs/protocol","text":"podbox host-guest Unix socket protocol — wire format, handshake, message types, capability negotiation, and notify action callbacks."},{"kind":"section","title":"Wire Format","page":"Host-Guest Socket Protocol","path":"/docs/protocol#wire-format","text":"Length-prefixed JSON over a Unix stream socket:"},{"kind":"section","title":"Socket Location","page":"Host-Guest Socket Protocol","path":"/docs/protocol#socket-location","text":"Socket Path Created by · Host socket $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/podbox/ .sock .socket Quadlet unit · Local guest socket /run/podbox/guest- .sock podbox-guest --daemon The host socket is created by systemd before the container starts and persists across restarts. The guest socket is used by interceptor processes to communicate with the local daemon."},{"kind":"section","title":"Handshake","page":"Host-Guest Socket Protocol","path":"/docs/protocol#handshake","text":"Guest sends: {   \"type\": \"hello\",   \"version\": \"0.1.0\",   \"container\": \"myenv\",   \"capabilities\": [\"notify\", \"xdg_open\", \"clipboard\", \"host_exec\"] } Host responds: {   \"type\": \"hello_ack\",   \"accepted\": [\"notify\", \"xdg_open\"],   \"rejected\": [\"clipboard\", \"host_exec\"],   \"idle_timeout_secs\": 0 } The handshake decides which capabilities the guest may use (0 timeout = disabled). The guest only installs interceptor symlinks for accepted ones."},{"kind":"section","title":"Message Types","page":"Host-Guest Socket Protocol","path":"/docs/protocol#message-types","text":""},{"kind":"section","title":"Guest → Host","page":"Host-Guest Socket Protocol","path":"/docs/protocol#guest-host","text":"Type Fields · hello protocol_version, guest_version, container, capabilities · notify summary, body, urgency, actions (optional), app_name (optional) · xdg_open uri · clipboard_set text (at most 8 MiB; larger content gets an error reply and leaves the session open) · clipboard_get — (the host answers error when the selection exceeds 8 MiB) · host_exec cmd, args · host_exec_v2 cmd, args, env (optional key/value pairs; only TERM, LANG, COLORTERM pass), cwd (optional guest working directory; interpreted per the configured cwd handling, defaulting to the host $HOME), tty (optional; see TTY sessions below) — the child's stdin/stdout/stderr arrive as three descriptors via SCM_RIGHTS on the same connection · host_exec_signal sig (optional signal number to relay to a running fd-passing host-exec child) · register_session — (pidfd via SCM_RIGHTS) · busy — · idle_timeout —"},{"kind":"section","title":"Host → Guest","page":"Host-Guest Socket Protocol","path":"/docs/protocol#host-guest","text":"Type Fields · hello_ack accepted, rejected, idle_timeout_secs · clipboard_data text · host_exec_stdout data · host_exec_stderr data · host_exec_output stream (stdout/stderr), data (raw bytes) — chunked streaming output, only when the guest negotiated host_exec_stream · host_exec_done exit_code, signal (optional; the killing signal when the child died of one — exit_code is then 128 + signal) · notify_action_result notification_id, action_key · ping — · check_idle — · shutdown —"},{"kind":"section","title":"Notify actions","page":"Host-Guest Socket Protocol","path":"/docs/protocol#notify-actions","text":"actions is an optional array of {key, label}. The host replies with notify_action_result carrying the notification_id and the chosen action_key. {   \"type\": \"notify\",   \"summary\": \"Build complete\",   \"body\": \"Exit code: 0\",   \"actions\": [     { \"key\": \"open\", \"label\": \"Open project\" },     { \"key\": \"dismiss\", \"label\": \"Dismiss\" }   ] Older guests omit actions/app_name — both default empty server-side."},{"kind":"section","title":"Capabilities","page":"Host-Guest Socket Protocol","path":"/docs/protocol#capabilities","text":"One interceptor symlink per capability. Rejected ones are skipped silently — no symlink, no retries. Capability Interceptor Description · notify notify-send Desktop notification forwarding · xdg_open xdg-open URI opening via host · clipboard podbox-clipboard Clipboard sharing · host_exec host-exec Execute commands on host · host_exec_stream — (requested by host-exec alongside host_exec) Chunked raw-byte output streaming; without it the host buffers one frame per pipe · host_exec_fds — (requested by host-exec alongside host_exec) Attached-stdio sessions: host_exec_v2 requests carry the child's three stdio descriptors via SCM_RIGHTS, so bytes flow through the pipes themselves with no protocol copying; host_exec_signal relays signals to the child A guest that predates host_exec_stream never requests it and keeps receiving buffered host_exec_stdout/host_exec_stderr frames, and it parses the "},{"kind":"section","title":"TTY sessions","page":"Host-Guest Socket Protocol","path":"/docs/protocol#tty-sessions","text":"A fd-passing host_exec_v2 request with tty = true carries the slave end of the guest's own pty three times (one descriptor per stdio slot). The guest allocates the pty when its stdout is a terminal, copies its terminal's termios and window size onto the slave, and keeps the master open until the session's exit report arrives — dropping it hangs the terminal up. The host runs the child as the new session's leader: setsid gives it a process group of its own (the same group host_exec_signal targets), and TIOCSCTTY on the inherited stdin makes the pty its controlling terminal, so the kernel delivers Ctrl-C, job control and window-size changes on the child directly, the way an interactive shell expects. An error in that setup aborts the spawn, and the guest sees the same typed failure a missing command does. Everything else on the session is unchanged: the cwd policy, the allowlist and argume"},{"kind":"key","title":"ImageTag","page":"Quadlet reference","path":"/docs/quadlet#build-file","text":"localhost/podbox- :latest · Local tag for built image"},{"kind":"key","title":"File","page":"Quadlet reference","path":"/docs/quadlet#build-file","text":"Absolute path to Containerfile · Must be absolute"},{"kind":"key","title":"Retry","page":"Quadlet reference","path":"/docs/quadlet#build-file","text":"From image.pull_retry · Pull retry count (default 3)"},{"kind":"key","title":"RetryDelay","page":"Quadlet reference","path":"/docs/quadlet#build-file","text":"From image.pull_retry_delay · Pull retry delay (default 5s)"},{"kind":"key","title":"ListenStream","page":"Quadlet reference","path":"/docs/quadlet#socket-file","text":"%t/podbox/ .sock · %t = $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR"},{"kind":"key","title":"Service","page":"Quadlet reference","path":"/docs/quadlet#socket-file","text":"-host.service · Activates the host socket server"},{"kind":"key","title":"SocketMode","page":"Quadlet reference","path":"/docs/quadlet#socket-file","text":"0600 · User-only access"},{"kind":"key","title":"DirectoryMode","page":"Quadlet reference","path":"/docs/quadlet#socket-file","text":"0700 · Parent dir permissions"},{"kind":"key","title":"Unit.Description","page":"Quadlet reference","path":"/docs/quadlet#unit","text":"podbox -- · Human-readable name"},{"kind":"key","title":"Unit.Requires","page":"Quadlet reference","path":"/docs/quadlet#unit","text":".socket · Socket must be available"},{"kind":"key","title":"Unit.After","page":"Quadlet reference","path":"/docs/quadlet#unit","text":".socket · Socket starts first"},{"kind":"key","title":"Unit.Requires","page":"Quadlet reference","path":"/docs/quadlet#unit","text":"-proxy.service · D-Bus proxy (conditional on [dbus] rules)"},{"kind":"key","title":"Unit.After","page":"Quadlet reference","path":"/docs/quadlet#unit","text":"-proxy.service · Proxy starts first (conditional)"},{"kind":"key","title":"Unit.Requires","page":"Quadlet reference","path":"/docs/quadlet#unit","text":"-compositor.service · Wayland firewall (conditional on Wayland proxy)"},{"kind":"key","title":"Unit.After","page":"Quadlet reference","path":"/docs/quadlet#unit","text":"-compositor.service · Compositor starts first (conditional)"},{"kind":"key","title":"Unit.Wants","page":"Quadlet reference","path":"/docs/quadlet#unit","text":"-x11.service · Optional X11 bridge (conditional on integration.x11)"},{"kind":"key","title":"Unit.After","page":"Quadlet reference","path":"/docs/quadlet#unit","text":"-x11.service · X11 bridge is started before the container (conditional)"},{"kind":"key","title":"Unit.Requires","page":"Quadlet reference","path":"/docs/quadlet#unit","text":"User-defined from [systemd].requires · Custom dependencies"},{"kind":"key","title":"Unit.After","page":"Quadlet reference","path":"/docs/quadlet#unit","text":"User-defined from [systemd].after · Custom ordering"},{"kind":"key","title":"Unit.StartLimitBurst","page":"Quadlet reference","path":"/docs/quadlet#unit","text":"5 · Max restarts in interval"},{"kind":"key","title":"Unit.StartLimitIntervalSec","page":"Quadlet reference","path":"/docs/quadlet#unit","text":"30s · Restart burst window"},{"kind":"key","title":"Container.Image","page":"Quadlet reference","path":"/docs/quadlet#container","text":"Prebuilt ref or localhost/podbox- :latest · Prebuilt images use the registry ref directly; custom builds use local tag"},{"kind":"key","title":"Container.Retry","page":"Quadlet reference","path":"/docs/quadlet#container","text":"From image.pull_retry · Pull retries (prebuilt only)"},{"kind":"key","title":"Container.RetryDelay","page":"Quadlet reference","path":"/docs/quadlet#container","text":"From image.pull_retry_delay · Pull retry delay (prebuilt only)"},{"kind":"key","title":"Container.ContainerName","page":"Quadlet reference","path":"/docs/quadlet#container","text":"Podman container name"},{"kind":"key","title":"Container.UserNS","page":"Quadlet reference","path":"/docs/quadlet#container","text":"keep-id (default) · User namespace mode (configurable via security.userns: keep-id, nomap, private)"},{"kind":"key","title":"Container.User","page":"Quadlet reference","path":"/docs/quadlet#container","text":"root · Run as root inside the container (UID mapped via UserNS)"},{"kind":"key","title":"Container.SecurityLabelDisable","page":"Quadlet reference","path":"/docs/quadlet#container","text":"true · Required for Wayland socket access"},{"kind":"key","title":"Container.SeccompProfile","page":"Quadlet reference","path":"/docs/quadlet#container","text":"From security.seccomp · Seccomp profile (conditional, e.g. \"default\", \"unconfined\")"},{"kind":"key","title":"Container.NoNewPrivileges","page":"Quadlet reference","path":"/docs/quadlet#container","text":"true · Emitted when security.no_new_privileges = true (default). Set false to allow sudo, su, AUR helpers."},{"kind":"key","title":"Container.Memory","page":"Quadlet reference","path":"/docs/quadlet#container","text":"From container.memory · Memory limit (conditional, e.g. \"4G\")"},{"kind":"key","title":"Container.PodmanArgs=--cpus=","page":"Quadlet reference","path":"/docs/quadlet#container","text":"From container.cpus · CPU limit (conditional, e.g. \"0.5\" → --cpus=0.5; Quadlet has no CpuQuota key)"},{"kind":"key","title":"Container.ReadOnly","page":"Quadlet reference","path":"/docs/quadlet#container","text":"true · Read-only rootfs (conditional on security.read_only_rootfs)"},{"kind":"key","title":"Container.RunInit","page":"Quadlet reference","path":"/docs/quadlet#container","text":"true · Always (catatonit as PID 1 for zombie reaping)"},{"kind":"key","title":"Container.WorkingDir","page":"Quadlet reference","path":"/docs/quadlet#container","text":"/home/%u · Always (default working directory)"},{"kind":"key","title":"Container.AddCapability","page":"Quadlet reference","path":"/docs/quadlet#container","text":"From security.cap_preset + security.cap_add · Capabilities (conditional; Quadlet has no CpuQuota key, so --cpus still goes through PodmanArgs)"},{"kind":"key","title":"Container.AppArmor","page":"Quadlet reference","path":"/docs/quadlet#container","text":"From security.apparmor · AppArmor profile (conditional, e.g. \"unconfined\")"},{"kind":"key","title":"Container.AddDevice","page":"Quadlet reference","path":"/docs/quadlet#container","text":"/dev/dri · GPU (conditional on gpu=true or gpu=\"auto\" with DRI present)"},{"kind":"key","title":"Container.AddDevice","page":"Quadlet reference","path":"/docs/quadlet#container","text":"nvidia.com/gpu=all when a host CDI spec exists, else -/dev/nvidiactl, -/dev/nvidia0…-/dev/nvidia7, -/dev/nvidia-modeset, -/dev/nvidia-uvm, -/dev/nvidia-uvm-tools · NVIDIA GPU passthrough (conditional on gpu=\"nvidia\" or gpu=\"auto\" with NVIDIA present; - marks each fallback node optional, and doctor suggests nvidia-ctk cdi generate for full driver passthrough)"},{"kind":"key","title":"Container.Network","page":"Quadlet reference","path":"/docs/quadlet#container","text":"From network.mode · Network mode (default host)"},{"kind":"key","title":"Container.PublishPort","page":"Quadlet reference","path":"/docs/quadlet#container","text":"From network.ports · Port mapping (conditional, ignored in host mode)"},{"kind":"key","title":"Container.AutoUpdate","page":"Quadlet reference","path":"/docs/quadlet#container","text":"registry or local · Auto-update (conditional on lifecycle.auto_update; registry for prebuilt, local for custom)"},{"kind":"key","title":"Container.ReloadCmd","page":"Quadlet reference","path":"/docs/quadlet#container","text":"From container.reload_cmd · Reload command (conditional)"},{"kind":"key","title":"Container.Timezone","page":"Quadlet reference","path":"/docs/quadlet#container","text":"local · Timezone sync (conditional on host /etc/localtime; replaces the old bind-mount so zone-name lookups keep working)"},{"kind":"key","title":"Service.Restart","page":"Quadlet reference","path":"/docs/quadlet#service","text":"on-failure · Auto-restart on crash"},{"kind":"key","title":"Service.RestartSec","page":"Quadlet reference","path":"/docs/quadlet#service","text":"2s · Delay between restarts"},{"kind":"key","title":"Install.WantedBy","page":"Quadlet reference","path":"/docs/quadlet#install","text":"default.target · Only when lifecycle.autostart = true"},{"kind":"page","title":"Quadlet reference","page":"Quadlet reference","path":"/docs/quadlet","text":"Reference for the systemd Quadlet units generated by podbox — .container, .build, .socket, and companion .service files with all supported keys."},{"kind":"section","title":".build file","page":"Quadlet reference","path":"/docs/quadlet#build-file","text":"Key Value Notes · ImageTag localhost/podbox- :latest Local tag for built image · File Absolute path to Containerfile Must be absolute · Retry From image.pull_retry Pull retry count (default 3) · RetryDelay From image.pull_retry_delay Pull retry delay (default 5s)"},{"kind":"section","title":".socket file","page":"Quadlet reference","path":"/docs/quadlet#socket-file","text":"Key Value Notes · ListenStream %t/podbox/ .sock %t = $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR · Service -host.service Activates the host socket server · SocketMode 0600 User-only access · DirectoryMode 0700 Parent dir permissions"},{"kind":"section","title":".container file","page":"Quadlet reference","path":"/docs/quadlet#container-file","text":""},{"kind":"section","title":"[Unit]","page":"Quadlet reference","path":"/docs/quadlet#unit","text":"Key Value Notes · Description podbox -- Human-readable name · Requires .socket Socket must be available · After .socket Socket starts first · Requires -proxy.service D-Bus proxy (conditional on [dbus] rules) · After -proxy.service Proxy starts first (conditional) · Requires -compositor.service Wayland firewall (conditional on Wayland proxy) · After -compositor.service Compositor starts first (conditional) · Wants -x11.service Optional X11 bridge (conditional on integration.x11) · After -x11.service X11 bridge is started before the container (conditional) · Requires User-defined from [systemd].requires Custom dependencies · After User-defined from [systemd].after Custom ordering · StartLimitBurst 5 Max restarts in interval · StartLimitIntervalSec 30s Restart burst window"},{"kind":"section","title":"[Container]","page":"Quadlet reference","path":"/docs/quadlet#container","text":"Key Value Notes · Image Prebuilt ref or localhost/podbox- :latest Prebuilt images use the registry ref directly; custom builds use local tag · Retry From image.pull_retry Pull retries (prebuilt only) · RetryDelay From image.pull_retry_delay Pull retry delay (prebuilt only) · ContainerName  Podman container name · UserNS keep-id (default) User namespace mode (configurable via security.userns: keep-id, nomap, private) · User root Run as root inside the container (UID mapped via UserNS) · SecurityLabelDisable true Required for Wayland socket access · SeccompProfile From security.seccomp Seccomp profile (conditional, e.g. \"default\", \"unconfined\") · NoNewPrivileges true Emitted when security.no_new_privileges = true (default). Set false to allow sudo, su, AUR helpers. · Memory From container.memory Memory limit (conditional, e.g. \"4G\") · PodmanArgs=--cpus= From container.cpus CPU limit (condi"},{"kind":"section","title":"Environment= entries","page":"Quadlet reference","path":"/docs/quadlet#environment-entries","text":"Variable Source Notes · HOME /home/%u Always set · HOST_USER Host username Injected into container · HOST_UID %U (systemd) Host UID · HOST_GID %G (systemd) Host GID · PATH /run/podbox/bin:... Interceptor directory prepended · WAYLAND_DISPLAY From host env Wayland display (conditional) · DISPLAY :0 X11 display (conditional on integration.x11; explicit [container.env] DISPLAY wins) · XDG_RUNTIME_DIR %t Always set when Wayland is enabled · MOZ_ENABLE_WAYLAND 1 Wayland-native Firefox/Thunderbird (conditional) · PIPEWIRE_RUNTIME_DIR %t PipeWire runtime dir (conditional) · PULSE_SERVER unix:%t/pulse/native PulseAudio server (conditional) · DBUS_SESSION_BUS_ADDRESS Proxy or direct D-Bus address (conditional) · SSH_AUTH_SOCK /run/podbox/ssh-agent.sock SSH agent (conditional, Podman ≥ 5.6) · GPG_TTY /dev/pts/0 GPG TTY (conditional) · GNUPGHOME /run/podbox/gnupg GPG home (conditional) · LANG From "},{"kind":"section","title":"Volume= entries","page":"Quadlet reference","path":"/docs/quadlet#volume-entries","text":"Source Destination Mode Condition · /.flatpak-info /.flatpak-info ro Always (sandbox detection — tricks apps into using portals) · %h/containers/ /home/%u Z Always (isolated home) · XDG dirs /home/%u/ ro,z or z Per [integration.xdg_dirs] · %h/.themes /home/%u/.themes ro sync_themes + path exists · %h/.local/share/themes /home/%u/.local/share/themes ro sync_themes + path exists · %h/.icons /home/%u/.icons ro sync_icons + path exists · %h/.local/share/icons /home/%u/.local/share/icons ro sync_icons + path exists · %h/.fonts /home/%u/.fonts ro sync_fonts + path exists · %h/.local/share/fonts /home/%u/.local/share/fonts ro sync_fonts + path exists · /etc/timezone /etc/timezone ro File exists on host (Debian convention; /etc/localtime itself is set up via Timezone=local, not a bind-mount) · Wayland socket Wayland socket ro wayland = true · %t/pipewire-0 %t/pipewire-0 (none) audio = true + Pip"},{"kind":"section","title":"PodmanArgs=","page":"Quadlet reference","path":"/docs/quadlet#podmanargs","text":"Only settings with no native Quadlet key go through PodmanArgs=: Arg Notes · --cpus= From container.cpus (conditional; Quadlet has no CpuQuota key, and --cpus also takes fractions like 0.5)"},{"kind":"section","title":"[Service]","page":"Quadlet reference","path":"/docs/quadlet#service","text":"Key Value Notes · Restart on-failure Auto-restart on crash · RestartSec 2s Delay between restarts Quadlet runs containers with --rm --replace, so every box is ephemeral: stopping one removes it, and persistent state lives in the home volume and snapshots. (lifecycle.on_stop is deprecated and emits nothing.)"},{"kind":"section","title":"[Install]","page":"Quadlet reference","path":"/docs/quadlet#install","text":"Key Value Notes · WantedBy default.target Only when lifecycle.autostart = true"},{"kind":"section","title":"Companion .service files","page":"Quadlet reference","path":"/docs/quadlet#companion-service-files","text":""},{"kind":"section","title":"<name>-host.service","page":"Quadlet reference","path":"/docs/quadlet#name-hostservice","text":"Generated at /.config/containers/systemd/ -host.service: [Unit] Description=podbox host socket server -- <name>  [Service] Type=simple ExecStart=<podbox> serve <name> Restart=on-failure RestartSec=2s"},{"kind":"section","title":"<name>-proxy.service (D-Bus proxy)","page":"Quadlet reference","path":"/docs/quadlet#name-proxyservice-d-bus-proxy","text":"Generated when [dbus] talk/own rules are configured. See dbus-proxy.md for details."},{"kind":"section","title":"<name>-compositor.service (Wayland firewall)","page":"Quadlet reference","path":"/docs/quadlet#name-compositorservice-wayland-firewall","text":"Generated when wayland.firewall = true (default). Runs podbox compositor to filter Wayland protocol access."},{"kind":"section","title":"<name>-x11.service (xwayland-satellite)","page":"Quadlet reference","path":"/docs/quadlet#name-x11service-xwayland-satellite","text":"Generated when integration.x11 = true. The host-side satellite connects to the same filtered or direct Wayland socket used by the container and exposes an isolated :0 X socket directory: [Unit] Wants=<name>-x11.service After=<name>-x11.service  [Service] Type=notify ExecStart=<xwayland-satellite> :0 Environment=WAYLAND_DISPLAY=<derived Wayland socket> The companion unit is optional at runtime: the container can start if the satellite is unavailable, but X11 applications will not work."},{"kind":"section","title":"Podman Version Targeting","page":"Quadlet reference","path":"/docs/quadlet#podman-version-targeting","text":"Podbox targets Podman 5.5+ with feature gating at 5.6 and 6.0. Feature Podman 5.5.x Podman ≥ 5.6, < 6.0 Podman ≥ 6.0 · ssh_agent Volume= + Environment=SSH_AUTH_SOCK on all versions Volume= + Environment=SSH_AUTH_SOCK on all versions Volume= + Environment=SSH_AUTH_SOCK on all versions · Quadlet install Manual copy into /.config/containers/systemd/ podman quadlet install --replace with file args (flat layout) podman quadlet install --replace --application (app-subdir layout) · Quadlet uninstall Manual file remove + systemctl daemon-reload podman quadlet rm .container (flat) podman quadlet rm --recursive (app-subdir) + flat fallback · Container list podman ps --filter label=podbox. podman quadlet list podman quadlet list Layout by version: 5.6–5.x: Units at …/systemd/ .container (flat). · 6.0+: Units at …/systemd/ / .container (app-subdir via --application). Status, doctor, and start probes"},{"kind":"section","title":"Important Notes","page":"Quadlet reference","path":"/docs/quadlet#important-notes","text":"%t is the systemd specifier for $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR — never substitute it. · %h is the systemd specifier for the user's home — never substitute it. · %U / %G expand to the user's UID and GID at unit start time. · Quadlet files (.container, .build) go in /.config/containers/systemd/. - Podman 5.6–5.x: flat layout ( .container). - Podman 6.0+: app-subdir layout ( / .container). · Custom systemd units (.socket, -host.service, etc.) always go in /.config/systemd/user/. · Re-running podbox enable is safe — it uses --replace to overwrite existing Quadlet files idempotently."},{"kind":"key","title":"dotfiles.source","page":"Home Setup","path":"/docs/setup#dotfiles","text":"string · required · host: to copy a local directory, or a Git URL/reference to clone"},{"kind":"key","title":"dotfiles.target","page":"Home Setup","path":"/docs/setup#dotfiles","text":"string · /.dotfiles · Destination inside the container home; must stay within that home"},{"kind":"key","title":"dotfiles.clone_on","page":"Home Setup","path":"/docs/setup#dotfiles","text":"string · \"host\" · Git clone location: \"host\" (reuses your SSH agent) or \"container\" (keeps the host untouched)"},{"kind":"key","title":"dotfiles.install","page":"Home Setup","path":"/docs/setup#dotfiles","text":"string · — · Shell command run inside the container from target after acquisition"},{"kind":"key","title":"provision..name","page":"Home Setup","path":"/docs/setup#provision","text":"string · required · Unique per container. ^[a-z0-9][a-z0-9_-]{0,63}$. dotfiles is reserved"},{"kind":"key","title":"provision..run","page":"Home Setup","path":"/docs/setup#provision","text":"string · required · Shell script, executed with sh -c. Single-line \"...\" or multi-line \"\"\"...\"\"\""},{"kind":"key","title":"provision..timeout","page":"Home Setup","path":"/docs/setup#provision","text":"string · \"5m\" · Duration: 30s, 5m, 1h. Must be > 0"},{"kind":"key","title":"provision..on_failure","page":"Home Setup","path":"/docs/setup#provision","text":"string · \"warn\" · \"warn\" prints and continues; \"abort\" stops the run"},{"kind":"key","title":"provision..env","page":"Home Setup","path":"/docs/setup#provision","text":"table · {} · Extra variables for this step. Keys can't start with PODBOX"},{"kind":"key","title":"provision..root","page":"Home Setup","path":"/docs/setup#provision","text":"bool · false · Run the step as root inside the container"},{"kind":"page","title":"Home Setup","page":"Home Setup","path":"/docs/setup","text":"One-shot setup inside a podbox container home — dotfiles and provisioning steps, both stamped, plus host-side automation."},{"kind":"section","title":"[dotfiles]","page":"Home Setup","path":"/docs/setup#dotfiles","text":"Populate the container home from a directory on the host or a Git URL. [dotfiles] source  = \"host:~/.dotfiles\" target  = \"~/.dotfiles\" install = \"./install.sh\" Key Type Default Description · source string required host: to copy a local directory, or a Git URL/reference to clone · target string /.dotfiles Destination inside the container home; must stay within that home · clone_on string \"host\" Git clone location: \"host\" (reuses your SSH agent) or \"container\" (keeps the host untouched) · install string — Shell command run inside the container from target after acquisition Host sources are copied in, so edits on the host arrive on the next dotfiles sync. Git sources clone on the host by default, which is why private repositories just work. Command What it does · podbox dotfiles sync [name] Re-acquire files and rerun the install command · podbox dotfiles status [name] Source, target, whethe"},{"kind":"section","title":"[[provision]]","page":"Home Setup","path":"/docs/setup#provision","text":"An ordered list of one-shot setup commands. This is [dotfiles] install generalized: as many steps as you need, each tracked independently. [[provision]] name = \"rust-toolchain\" run  = \"mise use -g rust@stable\"  [[provision]] name = \"git-defaults\" run = \"git config --global init.defaultBranch main\" on_failure = \"abort\" Key Type Default Description · name string required Unique per container. ^[a-z0-9][a-z0-9_-]{0,63}$. dotfiles is reserved · run string required Shell script, executed with sh -c. Single-line \"...\" or multi-line \"\"\"...\"\"\" · timeout string \"5m\" Duration: 30s, 5m, 1h. Must be > 0 · on_failure string \"warn\" \"warn\" prints and continues; \"abort\" stops the run · env table {} Extra variables for this step. Keys can't start with PODBOX · root bool false Run the step as root inside the container At most 64 steps. Order is declared order; steps run sequentially with no dependency gra"},{"kind":"section","title":"How steps are stamped","page":"Home Setup","path":"/docs/setup#how-steps-are-stamped","text":"When a step succeeds, podbox writes a stamp holding a hash of exactly what produces that step's result: hashed: name, run, root, env · not hashed: timeout, on_failure — they change how a step runs, not what it produces Next time, the hash is compared. A match skips the step; a mismatch marks it stale and it waits for an explicit run. It is never re-run silently. Change run, add an env entry, flip root, or rename a step and it goes stale. Tweak timeout and nothing happens."},{"kind":"section","title":"Running steps","page":"Home Setup","path":"/docs/setup#running-steps","text":"Command What it does · podbox create Runs dotfiles, then every pending/stale step · podbox provision sync [name] Runs pending + stale steps. Exits 1 if any step failed · podbox provision sync --force Re-runs everything, including applied steps · podbox provision sync --step toolchain --step git-defaults Runs only the named steps · podbox provision sync --dry-run Prints the plan, runs nothing · podbox provision status [name] State of every step, plus orphaned stamps · podbox provision status --check Exits 1 when anything is pending or stale — good for scripts · podbox provision status --output json {\"steps\": [{\"name\": ..., \"state\": ..., \"applied_at\": ...}]}"},{"kind":"section","title":"Step states","page":"Home Setup","path":"/docs/setup#step-states","text":"State Meaning · pending Declared in config, no stamp yet · applied Stamp hash matches the current step · stale Stamp exists but the hash differs — run provision sync · orphaned Stamp on disk, no step with that name in config. Reported only · unknown Stamps couldn't be read Stamps live in /.local/state/podbox/provision/ .stamp inside the container home, so their lifetime matches the home's: podbox remove keeps them (a recreate won't re-run), podbox remove --all deletes them (a recreate re-runs everything). podbox clone starts from a new home, so every step is pending again."},{"kind":"section","title":"Failure handling","page":"Home Setup","path":"/docs/setup#failure-handling","text":"on_failure = \"warn\" (the default) prints the failure and continues with the next step. The container stays usable. on_failure = \"abort\" stops the run immediately. During create the command exits 1 and the container is kept — not rolled back — so you can debug before retrying. provision sync exits 1 on any failure, warn included. podman exec [--user root] --workdir <home> -e K=V ... <container> sh -c <run> stdin is /dev/null, no TTY, output streams live prefixed with the step name · Plain non-login sh -c. Fish, mise shims and bash profiles are not sourced — use bash -lc '...' or absolute paths when you need them · timeout wraps the command; on expiry the step is a failure (the in-container child may linger, which is why long-running work belongs in [container.services]) · The container must be running; provision sync starts it if needed · env values are plaintext config — never put a secr"},{"kind":"section","title":"Sharing setup between configs","page":"Home Setup","path":"/docs/setup#sharing-setup-between-configs","text":"Both are guest-only, so both are safe to inherit through extends and bundled profiles. Provision steps merge by name: A new name appends · A child step with the same name replaces the parent's step in the parent's position So a shared base can declare toolchain, and every child can override it without reordering the run."},{"kind":"section","title":"Host-side automation","page":"Home Setup","path":"/docs/setup#host-side-automation","text":"Setup only ever runs inside the container. To run something on the host around a container's lifecycle, use a standard systemd drop-in on the generated unit. This is local policy, not part of the portable definition, on purpose. # ~/.config/systemd/user/<name>.service.d/10-local.conf [Service] ExecStartPre=/home/user/bin/check-nas.sh ExecStartPost=-/home/user/bin/notify-up.sh ExecStopPost=-/home/user/bin/cleanup.sh Why .service.d/ and not a Quadlet .container.d/: it targets the generated service by name, so it keeps working across podbox's flat (Podman 5.6–5.x) and app-subdir (6.0+) layouts, and podbox's install and uninstall paths never have to know about it. A - prefix makes a hook non-fatal · Hooks must be idempotent: Restart=on-failure re-runs them, and StartLimitBurst=5 caps retries · There is no pre-stop — systemd has no ExecStopPre · ExecStartPost fires when the container process "},{"kind":"section","title":"Common questions","page":"Home Setup","path":"/docs/setup#common-questions","text":"Editing a provision step trigger a rebuild? No. Steps are excluded from the build lock hash, so changing one only affects the home. Can I re-run a single step? podbox provision sync --step . It runs whether the step is applied, stale, or pending. Can setup touch the host? No. Everything here runs inside the container. That's also why it's safe to inherit through extends and bundled profiles. Can I do per-start one-shot work? Use a [container.services] entry with restart = \"never\". A provision step is a one-shot process, not a boot hook. Does podbox provision sync auto-start a stopped container? Yes, same as podbox dotfiles sync. provision status works while stopped — it reads stamps from the host."},{"kind":"page","title":"Troubleshooting","page":"Troubleshooting","path":"/docs/troubleshooting","text":"Fix common podbox issues — find your symptom, run the fix."},{"kind":"section","title":"Quick recovery","page":"Troubleshooting","path":"/docs/troubleshooting#quick-recovery","text":"Won't start? Run this first: podbox recover [NAME]        # guided fix; --yes skips prompts Safe and idempotent: reloads systemd, reinstalls Quadlets, rebuilds the image only if missing, then restarts. Never touches your home or config — only podbox remove --all deletes those. podbox doctor groups checks into Host / Container / Integration and ends with a plain-language Host exposure summary (network mode, D-Bus rules, clipboard, agents, host-exec allowlist, extra mounts). Exits non-zero when anything fails, so scripts can gate on it. podbox recover walks four steps — daemon-reload + reset-failed, Quadlet reinstall, image rebuild (only when missing), stop/start — confirming each on a TTY."},{"kind":"section","title":"Container won't start","page":"Troubleshooting","path":"/docs/troubleshooting#container-wont-start","text":"podman ps -a --filter name=<name>      # check container state podbox logs                            # container output podbox enable --dry-run                # inspect Quadlets without writing podbox enable                          # safe to re-run (uses --replace) If Quadlets are installed, also: systemctl --user status <name>.service"},{"kind":"section","title":"D-Bus proxy fails or container hangs on startup","page":"Troubleshooting","path":"/docs/troubleshooting#d-bus-proxy-fails-or-container-hangs-on-startup","text":"xdg-dbus-proxy is missing. Install it, or turn D-Bus off: which xdg-dbus-proxy   # should print a path [integration] dbus = false           # if you don't need D-Bus"},{"kind":"section","title":"GUI apps don't appear / Wayland socket errors","page":"Troubleshooting","path":"/docs/troubleshooting#gui-apps-dont-appear-wayland-socket-errors","text":"The socket path is baked in at podbox enable time. If it changed (e.g. after a reboot), regenerate: echo $WAYLAND_DISPLAY                    # should print wayland-0 or similar podbox enable                            # regenerate Quadlets (idempotent) podbox stop && podbox start"},{"kind":"section","title":"X11 apps don't start","page":"Troubleshooting","path":"/docs/troubleshooting#x11-apps-dont-start","text":"For X11 support, enable Wayland and install both host dependencies: xwayland-satellite and Xwayland. Then run: podbox doctor systemctl --user status <name>-x11.service If the satellite is missing, install it with your distribution's package manager or cargo install xwayland-satellite where no package is available, then rerun podbox enable. The X11 bridge is per-container; X11 clients in a container share its display, but the bridge does not use the host X socket."},{"kind":"section","title":"Interceptors not working","page":"Troubleshooting","path":"/docs/troubleshooting#interceptors-not-working","text":"notify-send, xdg-open, clipboard, and host-exec all go through the guest daemon. If it can't reach the host socket, they're silently skipped. podbox exec -- ps aux | grep podbox-guest       # daemon running? podbox exec -- echo $PATH                       # should include /run/podbox/bin podbox exec -- cat /etc/environment.d/podbox.conf   # PATH injection file"},{"kind":"section","title":"UID mismatch or permission errors","page":"Troubleshooting","path":"/docs/troubleshooting#uid-mismatch-or-permission-errors","text":"Host UID 1000 maps to container UID 999 (UserNS=keep-id, shifted by 1). Never chown a bind-mounted dir from inside the container — it changes ownership on the host too. · Files owned by nobody? The mount predates the UID mapping. Stop the container, fix ownership on the host, start again."},{"kind":"section","title":"SSH agent not forwarding","page":"Troubleshooting","path":"/docs/troubleshooting#ssh-agent-not-forwarding","text":"Needs Podman ≥ 5.6 and: [integration] ssh_agent = true podbox doctor                             # checks Podman version grep ssh_agent ~/.config/podbox/profiles/<name>.toml On Podman 5.5 the socket path is baked at enable time — if $SSH_AUTH_SOCK changed since (e.g. new login), re-run podbox disable && podbox enable."},{"kind":"section","title":"adb: no permissions / device unauthorized","page":"Troubleshooting","path":"/docs/troubleshooting#adb-no-permissions-device-unauthorized","text":"With [integration.hardware] usb = true: podbox doctor tells you whether any USB node is accessible to your user. If none is, install the device's udev rules (android-tools / android-udev for phones) or add yourself to the owning group (plugdev), then replug. · Only one adb server can claim a device: adb kill-server on the host before listing devices in the box. · \"unauthorized\" means the phone is waiting on the RSA prompt — accept it on the device and run adb devices again."},{"kind":"section","title":"Build fails or produces a stale image","page":"Troubleshooting","path":"/docs/troubleshooting#build-fails-or-produces-a-stale-image","text":"podbox build --rebuild Still broken? Clear the build context and rebuild: rm -rf ~/.local/share/podbox/<name>/ podbox build --rebuild Custom-build Containerfiles regenerate from TOML on every build — package and run.commands changes are picked up automatically."},{"kind":"section","title":"Container starts but podbox shell hangs","page":"Troubleshooting","path":"/docs/troubleshooting#container-starts-but-podbox-shell-hangs","text":"The shell in container.shell isn't installed in the image. Add it to [image.packages].install, then podbox build --rebuild."},{"kind":"section","title":"Commands target the wrong container","page":"Troubleshooting","path":"/docs/troubleshooting#commands-target-the-wrong-container","text":"Resolution order: positional [NAME] → -C → $PODBOX_CONTAINER → active context → picker → single config → embedded default. podbox use                  # show current context podbox use <name>           # set it podbox use --clear          # clear it"},{"kind":"section","title":"Mount propagation errors (nested mounts not visible)","page":"Troubleshooting","path":"/docs/troubleshooting#mount-propagation-errors-nested-mounts-not-visible","text":"Symptom: bind mounts or nested mounts created inside the container are not visible on the host, or podman reports mount-propagation errors. Cause: the root filesystem / is not marked shared, so mount events do not propagate across the mount namespace boundary. Fix: check the current propagation mode and make the root shared: findmnt -o PROPAGATION / sudo mount --make-rshared / To make it persistent across reboots, add the mount --make-rshared / step to your boot sequence (for example via a systemd oneshot unit)."},{"kind":"section","title":"Slow builds or overlay errors in rootless mode","page":"Troubleshooting","path":"/docs/troubleshooting#slow-builds-or-overlay-errors-in-rootless-mode","text":"Symptom: image builds are very slow, or fail with overlay/fuse-overlayfs errors when running rootless podman. Cause: podman is using the fuse-overlayfs storage driver instead of the kernel's native overlay, usually because the kernel is older than 5.13 or user-namespace support is disabled. Diagnose: podman info --format '{{.Store.GraphDriverName}}' Fix: upgrade to a kernel >= 5.13 with user namespaces enabled, then reset the storage: podman system reset After reset, podman info should report overlay as the graph driver."},{"kind":"section","title":"Exported app missing from the application menu","page":"Troubleshooting","path":"/docs/troubleshooting#exported-app-missing-from-the-application-menu","text":"Symptom: podbox export succeeded but the exported application does not appear in the desktop menu. Cause: the generated .desktop file fails TryExec (the binary is not on PATH inside the container or the shim is stale), or the desktop database is out of date. Fix: validate the exported file and refresh the database: desktop-file-validate ~/.local/share/applications/<name>.desktop update-desktop-database ~/.local/share/applications Also verify the TryExec line points at an executable that exists."},{"kind":"section","title":"Prompt shows \"I have no name!\"","page":"Troubleshooting","path":"/docs/troubleshooting#prompt-shows-i-have-no-name","text":"Symptom: a shell inside the container prints I have no name! instead of the username. Cause: the image creates a user whose UID collides with an existing UID in the image's /etc/passwd, so NSS cannot resolve the name. Diagnose inside the container: podman exec <container> getent passwd $UID Fix: rebuild with a non-colliding UID, or set container.user / the image's user explicitly in the TOML definition."},{"kind":"section","title":"setlocale warnings","page":"Troubleshooting","path":"/docs/troubleshooting#setlocale-warnings","text":"Symptom: commands print warnings such as bash: warning: setlocale: LC_ALL: cannot change locale. Cause: the locale requested by the host (or by [container.env]) is not generated inside the image. Diagnose inside the container: podman exec <container> locale -a Fix: add the locale packages to [image.packages].install (for example glibc-langpack-en on Fedora or locales on Debian/Ubuntu) and rebuild, or drop the offending LC_/LANG value from the config."},{"kind":"section","title":"Notifications, clipboard or xdg-open do nothing","page":"Troubleshooting","path":"/docs/troubleshooting#notifications-clipboard-or-xdg-open-do-nothing","text":"Symptom: notify-send, clipboard copy/paste or xdg-open inside the container silently does nothing. Cause: the capability was not negotiated during the guest↔host handshake, or the host socket service is down. Diagnose: systemctl --user status podbox-<name>.socket Fix: ensure the socket unit is active and re-run podbox enable . The guest sends a Hello on connect; a successful handshake is required before any interceptor request is served."},{"kind":"section","title":"GPU apps fail on NVIDIA","page":"Troubleshooting","path":"/docs/troubleshooting#gpu-apps-fail-on-nvidia","text":"Symptom: GPU-accelerated applications fail to start inside the container on an NVIDIA host. Cause: no CDI (Container Device Interface) specification was generated for the NVIDIA devices, so podman cannot inject them. Fix: generate the CDI spec and re-enable the container: sudo nvidia-ctk cdi generate --output=/etc/cdi/nvidia.yaml podbox enable <name> See the NVIDIA container toolkit documentation for details."}];
